/* Pharmacology 11: TB, fungal, viral, malaria, parasites */
Z("pharm11",0,"ph",["Tuberculosis is treated with a combination of drugs for at least 6 months — isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol — to kill actively dividing and dormant bacilli and prevent resistance; drug-resistant TB needs newer drugs like bedaquiline, linezolid and pretomanid.","TB bacilli live in different 'neighbourhoods' (fast-growing in cavities, slow in macrophages, dormant in caseum); each drug patrols a different one, and combinations stop resistant mutants escaping.",["First-line: isoniazid (H), rifampicin (R), pyrazinamide (Z), ethambutol (E), streptomycin (S); second-line: fluoroquinolones (levofloxacin, moxifloxacin), bedaquiline, linezolid, clofazimine, cycloserine, delamanid, pretomanid, amikacin, ethionamide, PAS.","Isoniazid: inhibits mycolic acid synthesis (InhA; activated by KatG); bactericidal to dividing bacilli; ADR — peripheral neuropathy (give pyridoxine), hepatitis, lupus, seizures; slow vs fast acetylators. Rifampicin: inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase; sterilising; ADR — hepatitis, orange-red secretions, flu-like syndrome, thrombocytopenia, potent enzyme inducer (OCPs, warfarin, ART).","NTEP regimen (drug-sensitive): 2HRZE + 4HRE (daily, weight-band FDC) — new and previously treated; DOTS with Nikshay; doses H 5, R 10, Z 25, E 15 mg/kg.","MDR/RR-TB: BPaLM (bedaquiline + pretomanid + linezolid + moxifloxacin) 6 months or shorter oral bedaquiline regimen; MAC — clarithromycin/azithromycin + ethambutol ± rifabutin."],"Pyridoxine with INH (neuropathy); rifampicin = 'red man' urine + enzyme inducer."],
"Tuberculosis, caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, requires prolonged multidrug therapy because of (1) slow growth and dormancy (persisters), (2) large bacterial populations in cavities with spontaneous drug-resistant mutants (combination prevents selection), (3) intracellular location. Bacterial subpopulations: rapidly multiplying extracellular bacilli in cavity walls (isoniazid most active, also rifampicin, streptomycin); slowly multiplying intracellular bacilli in acidic macrophages (pyrazinamide); spurters in caseous foci (rifampicin); dormant bacilli (no drug reliably). Classification: first-line — isoniazid (H), rifampicin (R), pyrazinamide (Z), ethambutol (E), streptomycin (S); second-line (WHO groups for MDR-TB): group A — levofloxacin/moxifloxacin, bedaquiline, linezolid; group B — clofazimine, cycloserine/terizidone; group C — ethambutol, delamanid, pyrazinamide, imipenem–cilastatin/meropenem, amikacin (streptomycin), ethionamide/prothionamide, PAS; new — pretomanid; others — rifabutin, rifapentine (latent TB — 3HP). Isoniazid (INH): mechanism — prodrug activated by mycobacterial catalase–peroxidase (KatG) to an isonicotinoyl radical that forms an adduct with NAD, inhibiting enoyl-ACP reductase (InhA) of the FAS-II system, blocking mycolic acid synthesis; bactericidal for rapidly dividing bacilli, bacteriostatic for resting; highly specific for M. tuberculosis; resistance — katG mutations (high-level), inhA promoter mutations (low-level, cross-resistance with ethionamide); pharmacokinetics — well absorbed orally, penetrates all tissues, CSF, caseous material and macrophages; metabolised by N-acetyltransferase 2 (NAT2) — genetic polymorphism: slow acetylators (more neuropathy, higher levels; ~50% of Indians) and fast acetylators (lower levels, possibly more hepatotoxicity from acetylhydrazine); t½ 1 h (fast) to 3 h (slow); uses — all forms of TB (first-line), latent TB infection (TB preventive therapy — 6H, or 3HP with rifapentine), contacts of active cases (children < 5, PLHIV); adverse effects — peripheral neuropathy (pyridoxine deficiency — INH forms hydrazone with pyridoxal and increases its excretion; prevented by pyridoxine 10–25 mg/day; more in slow acetylators, malnutrition, alcoholism, diabetes, HIV, pregnancy), hepatotoxicity (transaminase rise in 10–20%, clinical hepatitis 1%; risk increases with age, alcohol, rifampicin, pre-existing liver disease), optic neuritis, convulsions, psychosis, memory impairment, drug-induced lupus (ANA positive), rashes, fever, pellagra, sideroblastic anaemia, gynaecomastia; inhibits CYP2C19/3A4 — raises phenytoin, carbamazepine, diazepam levels; overdose causes refractory seizures and metabolic acidosis — treat with high-dose IV pyridoxine. Rifampicin: semisynthetic rifamycin; mechanism — binds β-subunit of bacterial DNA-dependent RNA polymerase (rpoB), inhibiting transcription initiation; bactericidal for intracellular and extracellular bacilli including slowly metabolising 'spurters' — key sterilising drug allowing 6-month therapy; spectrum — M. tuberculosis, M. leprae, MAC (rifabutin), staphylococci (including MRSA; not monotherapy), Neisseria meningitidis, H. influenzae, Legionella, Brucella, Chlamydia, some Gram-negatives, poxviruses; resistance — rpoB mutations (81-bp hotspot; detected by GeneXpert MTB/RIF/Truenat — RR-TB marker of MDR); pharmacokinetics — well absorbed orally (empty stomach), widely distributed including CSF (inflamed), caseous lesions, abscesses; metabolised by deacetylation (active metabolite), enterohepatic circulation; autoinduction; excreted in bile; t½ 2–5 h; imparts orange-red colour to urine, sweat, tears, saliva (stains contact lenses); uses — tuberculosis (all regimens), leprosy (monthly in MDT), prophylaxis of meningococcal (600 mg BD × 2 days) and H. influenzae type b contacts, brucellosis (with doxycycline), MRSA prosthetic device infections (with vancomycin), Legionella, latent TB (4R), cholestatic pruritus (enzyme induction); adverse effects — hepatotoxicity (cholestatic or hepatocellular; additive with INH, PZA), GI upset, rashes, flu-like syndrome (fever, chills, myalgia — with intermittent dosing), thrombocytopenia and purpura, haemolytic anaemia, acute renal failure (immune, intermittent use), respiratory syndrome, shock; drug interactions — most potent inducer of CYP3A4, 2C9, 2C19, 1A2 and P-gp: reduces efficacy of oral contraceptives (advise alternative contraception), warfarin, corticosteroids, sulfonylureas, azole antifungals, HIV protease inhibitors and NNRTIs (use rifabutin; efavirenz/dolutegravir double dose acceptable), methadone (withdrawal), digoxin, theophylline, phenytoin, calcium channel blockers, statins, cyclosporine, tacrolimus, bedaquiline. Pyrazinamide: prodrug converted by pyrazinamidase to pyrazinoic acid; active in acidic pH within macrophages and inflammatory sites; disrupts membrane energetics and trans-translation; sterilising in initial 2 months (shortens therapy to 6 months); ADR — hepatotoxicity (dose-related; most hepatotoxic first-line drug), hyperuricaemia and gout (inhibits urate excretion), arthralgia, GI upset, photosensitivity; M. bovis intrinsically resistant. Ethambutol: inhibits arabinosyltransferase (embB) — arabinogalactan synthesis; bacteriostatic; prevents emergence of resistance; ADR — retrobulbar (optic) neuritis with reduced visual acuity and red–green colour blindness (dose-related; test vision; caution in children who cannot report and renal failure), hyperuricaemia, peripheral neuropathy. Streptomycin: aminoglycoside; IM; extracellular bacilli; ototoxicity (vestibular), nephrotoxicity; now limited to selected regimens. Treatment regimens (National TB Elimination Programme, India): diagnosis by NAAT (CBNAAT/Truenat) with rifampicin resistance testing for all; drug-sensitive TB (new and previously treated, pulmonary and extrapulmonary) — intensive phase 2 months of HRZE daily followed by continuation phase 4 months of HRE daily (2HRZE/4HRE), using weight-band fixed-dose combinations (adult FDC: H 75 + R 150 + Z 400 + E 275 mg; 2–5 tablets by weight 25–75 kg); continuation phase extended (by 3–6 months) for TB meningitis, spinal TB, bone and joint TB (total 9–12 months; with corticosteroids for meningitis and pericarditis); daily dosing under supervision (DOT/99DOTS/family DOT), monitoring via Nikshay, nutritional support (Nikshay Poshan Yojana), pyridoxine supplementation; HIV co-infection — same regimen + ART within 2 weeks (8 weeks in TB meningitis), cotrimoxazole prophylaxis; pregnancy — HRZE (avoid streptomycin); liver disease — modified regimens; usual doses — INH 5 mg/kg (max 300 mg), rifampicin 10 mg/kg (max 600 mg), PZA 25 mg/kg, ethambutol 15 mg/kg. INH-resistant TB — 6 months of (R)ZE + levofloxacin. Drug-resistant TB: MDR-TB (resistant to H and R), RR-TB, pre-XDR (+ fluoroquinolone resistance), XDR (+ bedaquiline or linezolid); regimens — all-oral 6-month BPaLM (bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid, moxifloxacin) for MDR/RR-TB in ≥ 14 years (BPaL for pre-XDR); shorter oral bedaquiline-containing MDR/RR-TB regimen (9–11 months: bedaquiline, levofloxacin/moxifloxacin, clofazimine, ethionamide/linezolid, high-dose INH, PZA, ethambutol); longer individualised regimens (18–20 months) for XDR; monitor QT (bedaquiline, delamanid, moxifloxacin, clofazimine), myelosuppression and neuropathy (linezolid). Mycobacterium avium complex: in HIV — clarithromycin (or azithromycin) + ethambutol ± rifabutin; prophylaxis with azithromycin 1200 mg weekly when CD4 < 50 (if not starting ART promptly); pulmonary MAC — macrolide + rifampicin + ethambutol (three times weekly for nodular disease, daily for cavitary ± amikacin), continued 12 months after culture conversion; amikacin liposome inhalation for refractory disease.",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs"],["First-line","Isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol, streptomycin"],["Group A (MDR)","Levofloxacin/moxifloxacin, bedaquiline, linezolid"],["Group B","Clofazimine, cycloserine"],["Group C","Delamanid, amikacin, ethionamide, PAS, carbapenems"],["New","Pretomanid, rifapentine"]]],
["First-line drugs",[["Drug","MOA","Action","Key ADR"],["Isoniazid","Mycolic acid synthesis (InhA)","Cidal (dividing)","Neuropathy, hepatitis, lupus"],["Rifampicin","RNA polymerase (rpoB)","Cidal, sterilising","Hepatitis, orange secretions, flu-like, enzyme induction"],["Pyrazinamide","Pyrazinoic acid (acidic pH)","Cidal (intracellular)","Hepatotoxicity, hyperuricaemia"],["Ethambutol","Arabinosyltransferase","Static","Optic neuritis (red-green)"],["Streptomycin","30S ribosome","Cidal (extracellular)","Ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity"]]],
["NTEP regimens",[["Category","Regimen"],["Drug-sensitive (new/previously treated)","2HRZE + 4HRE daily (FDC by weight band)"],["CNS, bone, spinal TB","2HRZE + 7–10HRE (+ steroids for meningitis)"],["INH-resistant","6 months (R)ZE + levofloxacin"],["MDR/RR-TB","BPaLM 6 months or shorter oral Bdq regimen 9–11 months"],["Pre-XDR","BPaL; longer individualised regimen"],["Latent TB","6H, 3HP, 4R"]]],
["Doses (mg/kg/day)",[["Drug","Dose"],["Isoniazid","5 (max 300 mg)"],["Rifampicin","10 (max 600 mg)"],["Pyrazinamide","25"],["Ethambutol","15"],["Pyridoxine","10–25 mg/day"]]]],
{i:"India carries the highest TB burden globally; NTEP aims at TB elimination through early diagnosis by NAAT, daily FDC regimens and patient support.",x:[["MAC treatment",["Clarithromycin/azithromycin + ethambutol ± rifabutin","Prophylaxis in AIDS (CD4 < 50): azithromycin weekly","Pulmonary MAC: macrolide + rifampicin + ethambutol ± amikacin"]],["Rifampicin interactions",["Oral contraceptives (failure)","Warfarin, steroids, sulfonylureas","HIV protease inhibitors/NNRTIs (use rifabutin)","Azoles, methadone, cyclosporine, bedaquiline"]]],f:"Bacillary populations:\n Rapidly dividing (cavity) ← INH > R, S\n Intracellular acidic ← PZA\n Spurters (caseum) ← R\n Dormant ← none (persist)\n Combination → prevents resistance; 2HRZE (kill) + 4HRE (sterilise)",c:"Antitubercular therapy relies on combination regimens: INH and rifampicin form the backbone, pyrazinamide shortens therapy and ethambutol protects against resistance; drug-resistant TB now has effective all-oral shorter regimens."});

Z("pharm11",1,"ph",["Antifungal drugs act on the fungal cell membrane (ergosterol), cell wall or nucleic acid synthesis; amphotericin B (polyene) is for serious systemic mycoses, azoles (ketoconazole, fluconazole, itraconazole, voriconazole) are versatile oral agents, and griseofulvin is for dermatophyte infections of hair, skin and nails.","Fungal membranes contain ergosterol instead of cholesterol; amphotericin punches holes in it, azoles stop it being made, and griseofulvin jams the fungal cell division machinery.",["Classification: polyenes (amphotericin B, nystatin, natamycin), azoles — imidazoles (ketoconazole, clotrimazole, miconazole) and triazoles (fluconazole, itraconazole, voriconazole, posaconazole, isavuconazole), echinocandins (caspofungin, micafungin, anidulafungin), allylamines (terbinafine), antimetabolite (flucytosine), griseofulvin, topical (ciclopirox, tolnaftate, amorolfine).","Amphotericin B: binds ergosterol → pores → leakage (fungicidal); IV; uses — mucormycosis (drug of choice), cryptococcal meningitis (+ flucytosine), invasive aspergillosis, candidiasis, histoplasmosis, kala-azar; ADR — infusion reactions (fever, chills), nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, anaemia; lipid formulations less toxic.","Ketoconazole: inhibits lanosterol 14α-demethylase (CYP51); also inhibits human steroidogenesis — now used for Cushing's syndrome and topically; ADR — hepatotoxicity, gynaecomastia, impotence; needs acid; potent CYP3A4 inhibitor.","Griseofulvin: binds tubulin → disrupts mitotic spindle; deposits in keratin; fungistatic; uses — dermatophytoses (tinea capitis, corporis, nails); ineffective topically, Candida; ADR — headache, photosensitivity, hepatotoxicity, enzyme inducer (↓ OCP, warfarin), disulfiram-like reaction, teratogenic."],"Mucormycosis → amphotericin B; tinea capitis → griseofulvin/terbinafine."],
"Fungal infections range from superficial (dermatophytoses, candidiasis) to life-threatening systemic mycoses (candidaemia, aspergillosis, mucormycosis, cryptococcosis, histoplasmosis), increasing with immunosuppression, diabetes, ICU care and COVID-19 associated mucormycosis in India. Selective targets: ergosterol in the fungal cell membrane (absent in humans who have cholesterol), β-1,3-glucan in the fungal cell wall, fungal nucleic acid synthesis and microtubules. Classification: (1) antibiotics — polyenes: amphotericin B (conventional deoxycholate, liposomal, lipid complex), nystatin, natamycin, hamycin; heterocyclic benzofuran: griseofulvin; echinocandins: caspofungin, micafungin, anidulafungin, rezafungin; (2) antimetabolite — flucytosine (5-FC); (3) azoles — imidazoles: topical — clotrimazole, econazole, miconazole, oxiconazole, sertaconazole, luliconazole; systemic — ketoconazole; triazoles (systemic): fluconazole, itraconazole, voriconazole, posaconazole, isavuconazole; (4) allylamines — terbinafine, naftifine, butenafine; (5) other topical agents — tolnaftate, ciclopirox olamine, amorolfine, benzoic acid (Whitfield ointment), salicylic acid, undecylenic acid, selenium sulphide, sodium thiosulphate; (6) newer — ibrexafungerp, olorofim, fosmanogepix. Amphotericin B: polyene macrolide from Streptomyces nodosus; mechanism — binds ergosterol in fungal cell membrane forming transmembrane pores (micropores/sponges) through which K⁺, Na⁺, H⁺ and small molecules leak, causing cell death (fungicidal); also oxidative damage; lower affinity for cholesterol accounts for toxicity in human cells (kidney); spectrum — broadest: Candida (including most non-albicans), Cryptococcus, Aspergillus, Mucorales (Rhizopus, Mucor — drug of choice), Histoplasma, Blastomyces, Coccidioides, Paracoccidioides, Sporothrix; also Leishmania donovani and Naegleria; resistance rare (C. lusitaniae, C. auris variable, Aspergillus terreus, Scedosporium); pharmacokinetics — not absorbed orally (oral suspension for GI candidiasis), given IV by slow infusion (conventional 0.5–1 mg/kg/day over 4–6 h; liposomal 3–5 mg/kg/day, up to 10 mg/kg in mucormycosis), highly protein bound, poor CSF penetration (intrathecal in severe meningitis), long terminal t½ (15 days); not removed by dialysis; uses — mucormycosis (liposomal amphotericin B, with surgical debridement and control of diabetes), cryptococcal meningitis (induction with flucytosine, then fluconazole), invasive aspergillosis (when voriconazole contraindicated), invasive/disseminated candidiasis, candidaemia (alternative to echinocandins), endemic mycoses (severe histoplasmosis, blastomycosis, coccidioidomycosis, paracoccidioidomycosis), empirical therapy in febrile neutropenia, fungal endophthalmitis, kala-azar (visceral leishmaniasis — single-dose liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg is first-line in India), primary amoebic meningoencephalitis; topical — keratitis, oral thrush; adverse effects — (a) acute infusion-related reactions: fever, rigors, chills, headache, nausea, vomiting, hypotension, dyspnoea (cytokine release) — premedicate with paracetamol, antihistamine, hydrocortisone; test dose; pethidine for rigors; thrombophlebitis; (b) nephrotoxicity (dose-limiting; most patients): renal vasoconstriction and direct tubular toxicity — azotaemia, reduced GFR, renal tubular acidosis (distal), hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, nephrogenic DI; reduced by saline loading (500 mL–1 L before infusion), avoiding other nephrotoxins, lipid formulations; (c) normocytic normochromic anaemia (reduced erythropoietin), (d) arrhythmias with rapid infusion/hyperkalaemia, (e) neurotoxicity with intrathecal use; lipid formulations (liposomal — AmBisome; lipid complex — Abelcet) deliver drug preferentially to reticuloendothelial tissues, markedly reducing nephrotoxicity and infusion reactions — preferred, but expensive. Azoles: mechanism — inhibit fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme lanosterol 14α-demethylase (CYP51/ERG11), blocking conversion of lanosterol to ergosterol; accumulation of toxic 14α-methylsterols disrupts membrane function; fungistatic against yeasts (fungicidal against moulds for some triazoles); imidazoles (ketoconazole) are less selective and inhibit human CYP enzymes (steroidogenesis, drug metabolism) more than triazoles. Ketoconazole: first oral azole; requires gastric acid for absorption (reduced by antacids, H2 blockers, PPIs), highly protein bound, poor CSF penetration, hepatic metabolism, t½ 7–8 h (dose-dependent); uses — now limited for systemic fungal infections because of hepatotoxicity and interactions (replaced by triazoles); topical — dermatophytoses, pityriasis versicolor, seborrhoeic dermatitis and dandruff (2% shampoo/cream), cutaneous candidiasis; systemic — Cushing syndrome (inhibits adrenal steroidogenesis — CYP17, 11β-hydroxylase; levoketoconazole), advanced prostate cancer (androgen synthesis); adverse effects — GI upset, hepatotoxicity (raised transaminases, rarely fatal hepatitis — EMA/FDA restrictions), endocrine effects — gynaecomastia, oligospermia, impotence, loss of libido, menstrual irregularities, adrenal insufficiency (inhibits testosterone and cortisol synthesis), pruritus, rashes, teratogenic; interactions — potent CYP3A4 inhibitor (raises terfenadine, astemizole, cisapride — fatal arrhythmias; cyclosporine, warfarin, statins, midazolam, sildenafil), rifampicin and phenytoin reduce its levels. Triazoles: fluconazole — water-soluble, oral bioavailability > 90% (unaffected by acid), excellent CSF and urine penetration, renal excretion, t½ 30 h; active against Candida albicans (not C. krusei, reduced C. glabrata, C. auris often resistant), Cryptococcus, Coccidioides; not Aspergillus or Mucor; uses — oropharyngeal, oesophageal and vaginal candidiasis (single 150 mg dose), candidaemia (step-down), cryptococcal meningitis (consolidation and maintenance), prophylaxis in transplant/neutropenia, coccidioidomycosis, dermatophytoses, tinea versicolor; fewer endocrine effects; itraconazole — broad spectrum including Aspergillus, dermatophytes, Histoplasma, Blastomyces, Sporothrix; uses — onychomycosis (pulse therapy), dermatophytoses (widely used in India for recalcitrant tinea), endemic mycoses, allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis; negative inotrope (avoid in heart failure); voriconazole — drug of choice for invasive aspergillosis; visual disturbances, hallucinations, photosensitivity and skin cancer, hepatotoxicity, periostitis; posaconazole and isavuconazole — active against Mucorales (step-down/salvage therapy for mucormycosis), prophylaxis in high-risk haematological patients. Echinocandins (caspofungin, micafungin, anidulafungin): inhibit β-1,3-D-glucan synthase — cell wall synthesis; fungicidal for Candida (first-line for candidaemia and invasive candidiasis, including C. glabrata, C. auris), fungistatic for Aspergillus; IV only; well tolerated (histamine-like infusion reactions, hepatitis). Flucytosine: converted in fungi by cytosine deaminase to 5-fluorouracil, inhibiting thymidylate synthase and RNA function; synergistic with amphotericin B for cryptococcal meningitis; rapid resistance as monotherapy; bone marrow suppression, hepatotoxicity. Terbinafine: allylamine; inhibits squalene epoxidase (accumulation of toxic squalene; fungicidal); concentrates in keratin; drug of choice for onychomycosis (250 mg daily × 6 weeks fingernails, 12 weeks toenails) and dermatophytoses including tinea capitis (Trichophyton); ADR — GI upset, taste disturbance, hepatotoxicity, rashes, SJS. Griseofulvin: obtained from Penicillium griseofulvum; mechanism — enters susceptible dermatophytes by energy-dependent transport, binds tubulin and microtubule-associated proteins, disrupting mitotic spindle and inhibiting mitosis (metaphase arrest); also interferes with nucleic acid synthesis; fungistatic; deposited in keratin precursor cells of skin, hair and nails, making newly formed keratin resistant to fungal invasion — infected keratin is shed and replaced, so therapy must continue until complete replacement (weeks to months); spectrum — dermatophytes only (Trichophyton, Microsporum, Epidermophyton); inactive against Candida, Malassezia (pityriasis versicolor), bacteria and systemic fungi; pharmacokinetics — poorly water-soluble; absorption enhanced by fatty meals and ultramicrosize preparations; enzyme inducer; t½ 24 h; ineffective topically; uses — dermatophytoses: tinea capitis (drug of choice in children for Microsporum; 20–25 mg/kg/day microsize for 6–8 weeks), tinea corporis, cruris, pedis and barbae unresponsive to topical therapy (2–6 weeks), onychomycosis (6–12 months; low cure rates — terbinafine preferred); adverse effects — headache (common), GI upset, peripheral neuritis, mental confusion, fatigue, photosensitivity, urticaria, rashes, hepatotoxicity, leucopenia, porphyria exacerbation (contraindicated in porphyria), disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol, oestrogen-like effects in children; teratogenic and carcinogenic in animals — contraindicated in pregnancy, avoid conception for 1 month (women) and 6 months (men) after therapy; interactions — induces CYP enzymes: reduces efficacy of oral contraceptives and warfarin; phenobarbitone reduces griseofulvin absorption and levels.",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs","Target"],["Polyenes","Amphotericin B, nystatin, natamycin","Ergosterol (pores)"],["Imidazoles","Ketoconazole, clotrimazole, miconazole","14α-demethylase (ergosterol synthesis)"],["Triazoles","Fluconazole, itraconazole, voriconazole, posaconazole, isavuconazole","14α-demethylase"],["Echinocandins","Caspofungin, micafungin, anidulafungin","β-1,3-glucan synthase (cell wall)"],["Allylamines","Terbinafine","Squalene epoxidase"],["Antimetabolite","Flucytosine","DNA/RNA synthesis"],["Others","Griseofulvin","Microtubules (mitosis)"],["Topical","Ciclopirox, tolnaftate, amorolfine, Whitfield's","Various"]]],
["Amphotericin B vs ketoconazole vs griseofulvin",[["Feature","Amphotericin B","Ketoconazole","Griseofulvin"],["MOA","Binds ergosterol → pores","Inhibits ergosterol synthesis","Disrupts mitotic spindle"],["Action","Fungicidal","Fungistatic","Fungistatic"],["Route","IV","Oral, topical","Oral only"],["Spectrum","Broadest systemic","Yeasts, dermatophytes","Dermatophytes only"],["Main use","Mucormycosis, cryptococcal meningitis, kala-azar","Topical; Cushing's","Tinea capitis, dermatophytoses"],["Key ADR","Infusion reactions, nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia","Hepatotoxicity, gynaecomastia, CYP3A4 inhibition","Headache, photosensitivity, enzyme induction, teratogenic"]]],
["Drug of choice",[["Infection","Drug"],["Mucormycosis","Liposomal amphotericin B + surgery"],["Invasive aspergillosis","Voriconazole (isavuconazole)"],["Candidaemia","Echinocandin"],["Cryptococcal meningitis","Amphotericin B + flucytosine → fluconazole"],["Vaginal/oral candidiasis","Fluconazole, clotrimazole"],["Onychomycosis","Terbinafine (itraconazole pulse)"],["Tinea capitis","Griseofulvin / terbinafine"],["Pityriasis versicolor","Topical azole, selenium sulphide"]]],
["Amphotericin B toxicity",["Infusion reactions: fever, rigors, hypotension","Nephrotoxicity, RTA","Hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia","Anaemia","Thrombophlebitis","Reduced by saline loading, lipid formulations"]]],
{i:"Antifungal choice depends on the fungus, site and host; amphotericin B remains the broadest agent, azoles are the most versatile and echinocandins are first-line for candidaemia.",f:"Fungal targets:\n Ergosterol in membrane ← amphotericin B, nystatin (bind)\n Ergosterol synthesis: squalene → (squalene epoxidase ← terbinafine) → lanosterol → (14α-demethylase ← azoles) → ergosterol\n Cell wall glucan ← echinocandins\n DNA/RNA ← flucytosine; microtubules ← griseofulvin",c:"Amphotericin B is the broadest-spectrum fungicidal drug for serious mycoses but is nephrotoxic; ketoconazole has been replaced systemically by safer triazoles; griseofulvin acts only on dermatophytes via keratin deposition."});

Z("pharm11",2,"ph",["Antiviral drugs inhibit specific steps of viral replication; acyclovir (a nucleoside analogue) treats herpes simplex and varicella-zoster infections, while antiretroviral drugs such as zidovudine, tenofovir and dolutegravir are combined (ART) to suppress HIV.","Viruses use our cells as factories; antivirals target steps unique to the virus — like a fake building block (acyclovir) that only viral enzymes activate and then jams viral DNA chains.",["Classification: anti-herpes (acyclovir, valacyclovir, famciclovir, ganciclovir, valganciclovir, foscarnet, cidofovir), anti-influenza (oseltamivir, zanamivir, baloxavir, amantadine), anti-hepatitis (tenofovir, entecavir — HBV; sofosbuvir, velpatasvir, daclatasvir — HCV; interferons), antiretrovirals (NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, INSTIs, entry/fusion inhibitors), others (ribavirin, remdesivir, nirmatrelvir).","Acyclovir: phosphorylated by viral thymidine kinase → acyclo-GTP inhibits viral DNA polymerase, chain terminator; uses — HSV (genital, encephalitis IV 10 mg/kg 8-hourly, keratitis), varicella/zoster (800 mg 5×/day), prophylaxis; ADR — nephrotoxicity (crystalluria — hydrate), neurotoxicity, phlebitis.","Zidovudine (AZT, NRTI): phosphorylated by host kinases → inhibits reverse transcriptase, chain termination; ADR — bone marrow suppression (anaemia), myopathy, lactic acidosis, lipoatrophy; used in PPTCT (infant prophylaxis) and second-line.","ART (NACO 2021): first-line TLD — tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir for all; alternatives TLE (efavirenz); second-line — zidovudine + lamivudine + boosted PI (atazanavir/r or lopinavir/r) ± DTG; PEP — TLD × 28 days within 72 h; PPTCT — lifelong TLD to mother, infant nevirapine ± zidovudine 6–12 weeks."],"HIV ART: 'Treat all with TLD'; PEP within 72 h for 28 days."],
"Viruses are obligate intracellular parasites; antiviral drugs target virus-specific steps — attachment and entry, uncoating, genome replication (viral polymerases, reverse transcriptase), integration, protein processing (proteases), assembly and release (neuraminidase). Most are virustatic, active only against replicating virus, and do not eradicate latent infection. Classification: (1) anti-herpes virus — acyclovir, valacyclovir, famciclovir, penciclovir, ganciclovir, valganciclovir, foscarnet, cidofovir, idoxuridine, trifluridine, letermovir (CMV prophylaxis), maribavir; (2) anti-influenza — neuraminidase inhibitors (oseltamivir, zanamivir, peramivir), cap-dependent endonuclease inhibitor (baloxavir marboxil), M2 inhibitors (amantadine, rimantadine — widespread resistance); (3) anti-hepatitis B — tenofovir (TDF, TAF), entecavir, lamivudine, adefovir, telbivudine, pegylated interferon-α; anti-hepatitis C — direct-acting antivirals: NS5B polymerase inhibitor (sofosbuvir), NS5A inhibitors (daclatasvir, velpatasvir, ledipasvir), NS3/4A protease inhibitors (glecaprevir, grazoprevir); ribavirin; (4) antiretroviral (anti-HIV) — nucleoside/nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs: zidovudine, lamivudine, emtricitabine, abacavir, tenofovir, stavudine and didanosine — obsolete), non-nucleoside RTIs (NNRTIs: nevirapine, efavirenz, etravirine, rilpivirine, doravirine), protease inhibitors (PIs: lopinavir, atazanavir, darunavir — boosted with ritonavir/cobicistat), integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs: dolutegravir, raltegravir, bictegravir, cabotegravir), entry inhibitors (CCR5 antagonist maraviroc; fusion inhibitor enfuvirtide; attachment inhibitor fostemsavir; post-attachment ibalizumab), capsid inhibitor (lenacapavir); (5) non-selective/others — ribavirin (RSV, Lassa, HCV), interferons, remdesivir, nirmatrelvir–ritonavir, molnupiravir (COVID-19), imiquimod (HPV warts), palivizumab/nirsevimab (RSV prophylaxis). Acyclovir: guanosine analogue lacking 3'-hydroxyl (acyclic sugar); mechanism — selectively taken up by HSV/VZV-infected cells and monophosphorylated by viral thymidine kinase (TK) (100-fold more efficient than host), then converted by host kinases to acyclovir triphosphate, which competitively inhibits viral DNA polymerase (much more than host) and is incorporated into viral DNA causing chain termination (no 3'-OH); high selectivity and low toxicity; resistance — TK-deficient or altered mutants (immunocompromised), altered DNA polymerase; spectrum — HSV-1, HSV-2 > VZV (10× less sensitive — higher doses) > EBV; CMV insensitive (lacks TK); pharmacokinetics — low oral bioavailability (15–30%; valacyclovir prodrug 55%), widely distributed including CSF (50%), renal excretion by filtration and secretion (dose adjust), t½ 2–3 h; uses — (a) HSV: first episode genital herpes (400 mg TDS × 7–10 days), recurrent episodes (episodic) and suppressive therapy (400 mg BD), herpes labialis, herpes simplex encephalitis (IV 10 mg/kg 8-hourly × 14–21 days — start empirically; reduces mortality), neonatal herpes (IV 20 mg/kg 8-hourly), herpetic keratitis (3% ophthalmic ointment), herpetic whitlow, eczema herpeticum, mucocutaneous HSV in immunocompromised, prophylaxis in transplant recipients; (b) VZV: chickenpox in adolescents, adults, immunocompromised and pregnant women (800 mg 5 times daily × 7 days; IV in severe/pneumonia), herpes zoster (shingles) within 72 h — reduces acute pain, accelerates healing, may reduce post-herpetic neuralgia (800 mg 5 times daily × 7 days; valacyclovir 1 g TDS or famciclovir preferred), ophthalmic zoster, disseminated zoster (IV); (c) EBV-associated oral hairy leukoplakia; adverse effects — topical: burning, stinging; oral: well tolerated — nausea, diarrhoea, headache; IV: thrombophlebitis (alkaline pH), reversible nephrotoxicity — crystalline nephropathy (obstructive AKI with rapid infusion/dehydration; infuse over 1 h with hydration), neurotoxicity — tremor, confusion, hallucinations, seizures (elderly, renal impairment); safe in pregnancy; probenecid increases levels. Zidovudine (azidothymidine, AZT): thymidine analogue, the first antiretroviral (1987); mechanism — phosphorylated by host cellular kinases to zidovudine triphosphate, which competitively inhibits HIV reverse transcriptase and is incorporated into proviral DNA, causing chain termination (3'-azido group instead of hydroxyl); inhibits host mitochondrial DNA polymerase γ weakly (toxicity); active against HIV-1, HIV-2, HTLV; resistance — thymidine analogue mutations (TAMs) with monotherapy; pharmacokinetics — oral bioavailability 65%, crosses BBB and placenta, glucuronidated in liver, t½ 1 h (intracellular triphosphate 3–4 h); dose 300 mg BD; uses — HIV infection as part of combination ART (now mainly second-line in NACO: AZT + 3TC + boosted PI); prevention of parent-to-child transmission (infant prophylaxis with nevirapine ± AZT for 6–12 weeks in high-risk infants; intrapartum IV historically); post-exposure prophylaxis (older regimens); HIV-associated dementia and thrombocytopenia; adverse effects — bone marrow suppression (macrocytic anaemia, neutropenia — dose-limiting; monitor Hb, avoid if Hb < 8 g/dL), GI intolerance, headache, insomnia, fatigue, myopathy, nail and skin hyperpigmentation, lipoatrophy, hepatic steatosis and lactic acidosis (mitochondrial toxicity); interactions — ganciclovir, cotrimoxazole, other marrow suppressants (additive myelotoxicity), stavudine (antagonism — competition for phosphorylation), probenecid and fluconazole increase levels, rifampicin reduces. Antiretroviral therapy (NACO National Guidelines 2021): goals — durable viral suppression (undetectable viral load), immune restoration, reduced morbidity and mortality, prevention of transmission (U = U); 'Treat All' — ART initiated for all PLHIV regardless of CD4 count or clinical stage, ideally same day/within 7 days of diagnosis (delay in cryptococcal and TB meningitis); first-line regimen for adults and adolescents (including pregnant and breastfeeding women, TB co-infection): TLD — tenofovir disoproxil fumarate 300 mg + lamivudine 300 mg + dolutegravir 50 mg as a single daily fixed-dose tablet (with rifampicin, dolutegravir 50 mg twice daily); alternatives — TLE (tenofovir + lamivudine + efavirenz 400/600) when DTG cannot be used, abacavir + lamivudine + DTG (renal impairment), zidovudine + lamivudine + DTG (if TDF contraindicated); children — ABC + 3TC + DTG (paediatric DTG 10 mg dispersible) or ABC + 3TC + LPV/r; monitoring — viral load at 6 months and annually (failure: VL > 1000 copies/mL on two occasions after adherence counselling), CD4, renal function (TDF), weight; second-line after failure of DTG/NNRTI-based first-line: zidovudine + lamivudine + atazanavir/ritonavir (or lopinavir/ritonavir), or TDF/AZT + 3TC + DTG combinations depending on prior regimen; third-line — darunavir/ritonavir + dolutegravir ± optimised NRTIs (genotype-guided); adherence > 95%; prophylaxis — cotrimoxazole (CD4 < 350/WHO stage 3–4), TB preventive therapy (INH or 3HP), fluconazole for cryptococcal antigen positive; immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) may occur after starting ART. Post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP): for occupational (needlestick, mucosal splash) and non-occupational (sexual assault, condom rupture) exposures from a known or possibly HIV-positive source; first aid — wash wound with soap and water, do not squeeze or scrub; irrigate mucosae; assess exposure category and source status; start as early as possible, ideally within 2 hours and not later than 72 hours; regimen — TLD (tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir) once daily for 28 days (alternatives — TDF + 3TC + atazanavir/r or lopinavir/r); baseline and follow-up HIV testing at 6 weeks, 3 months (and 6 months); also hepatitis B vaccination/HBIG and HCV testing; counsel on safe sex and avoid blood donation during follow-up; pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) — TDF/FTC daily or event-driven, cabotegravir LA, lenacapavir. Perinatal prophylaxis (prevention of parent-to-child transmission, PPTCT): universal HIV testing of pregnant women; all HIV-positive pregnant and breastfeeding women receive lifelong TLD regardless of CD4 and gestation (start immediately — preferably before 14 weeks), viral load monitoring (aim for undetectable before delivery); safe delivery practices (avoid unnecessary instrumentation; elective caesarean only for obstetric indications or high VL in some guidelines); infant prophylaxis — syrup nevirapine for 6 weeks (low risk) or 12 weeks (high risk — mother not virally suppressed or ART < 24 weeks; dual prophylaxis nevirapine + zidovudine in some protocols) with exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months (avoid mixed feeding) while mother continues ART; cotrimoxazole prophylaxis for infant from 6 weeks until HIV status is ruled out; early infant diagnosis by DNA PCR at 6 weeks, 6 months, 12 months and antibody test at 18 months; transmission reduced from 30–45% to < 2%.",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs"],["Anti-herpes","Acyclovir, valacyclovir, famciclovir, ganciclovir, foscarnet, cidofovir"],["Anti-influenza","Oseltamivir, zanamivir, baloxavir, amantadine"],["Anti-HBV","Tenofovir, entecavir, lamivudine, interferon"],["Anti-HCV","Sofosbuvir, velpatasvir, daclatasvir, glecaprevir"],["NRTIs","Zidovudine, lamivudine, tenofovir, abacavir, emtricitabine"],["NNRTIs","Efavirenz, nevirapine, rilpivirine, doravirine"],["PIs","Atazanavir, lopinavir, darunavir (boosted)"],["INSTIs","Dolutegravir, raltegravir, bictegravir, cabotegravir"],["Entry/fusion","Maraviroc, enfuvirtide, fostemsavir"],["Others","Ribavirin, remdesivir, nirmatrelvir, interferons"]]],
["Acyclovir vs zidovudine",[["Feature","Acyclovir","Zidovudine"],["Analogue","Guanosine","Thymidine"],["Activation","Viral thymidine kinase (selective)","Host kinases"],["Target","Viral DNA polymerase","HIV reverse transcriptase"],["Uses","HSV, VZV infections; encephalitis","HIV (second-line), PPTCT infant prophylaxis"],["Key ADR","Nephrotoxicity (crystals), neurotoxicity","Anaemia, neutropenia, myopathy, lactic acidosis"]]],
["NACO ART regimens",[["Line","Regimen"],["First-line (adults, pregnancy, TB)","TLD: TDF + 3TC + DTG"],["Alternative first-line","TLE (efavirenz); ABC/AZT + 3TC + DTG"],["Children","ABC + 3TC + DTG (paediatric)"],["Second-line","AZT + 3TC + ATV/r or LPV/r (± DTG)"],["Third-line","DRV/r + DTG ± NRTIs (genotype-guided)"]]],
["PEP and PPTCT",[["Situation","Measures"],["PEP","Wash; start within 2 h (≤ 72 h); TLD × 28 days; test at baseline, 6 wk, 3 mo"],["Mother","Lifelong TLD from diagnosis; VL monitoring"],["Infant","Nevirapine 6 weeks (12 weeks / + AZT if high risk); cotrimoxazole from 6 weeks"],["Feeding","Exclusive breastfeeding 6 months"],["Diagnosis","DNA PCR 6 weeks, 6 months, 12 months; antibody 18 months"]]]],
{i:"Antivirals target virus-specific replication steps; modern ART with dolutegravir-based regimens can suppress HIV to undetectable levels, preventing disease progression and transmission.",x:[["HIV life cycle targets",["Attachment/entry — maraviroc, enfuvirtide","Reverse transcription — NRTIs, NNRTIs","Integration — INSTIs","Protease maturation — PIs","Capsid — lenacapavir"]]],f:"Acyclovir → (viral TK) → acyclo-MP → (host kinases) → acyclo-TP → inhibits viral DNA polymerase + chain termination\n HIV: RNA → (reverse transcriptase ← NRTIs, NNRTIs) → DNA → (integrase ← DTG) → provirus → polyprotein → (protease ← PIs) → mature virion",c:"Acyclovir is a selective, safe anti-herpes drug activated by viral thymidine kinase; zidovudine pioneered ART; and current NACO guidelines treat all PLHIV with TLD, use the same regimen for PEP, and combine maternal ART with infant prophylaxis to prevent perinatal transmission."});

Z("pharm11",3,"ph",["Antimalarial drugs target different stages of the malaria parasite's life cycle; chloroquine treats vivax malaria, primaquine eradicates liver hypnozoites (radical cure) and kills gametocytes, mefloquine is used for prophylaxis and resistant malaria, and artemisinin-based combination therapies (ACTs) are first-line for falciparum malaria.","Different drugs attack the parasite in different 'homes': blood schizonticides kill parasites in red cells (symptoms), tissue schizonticides clear the liver (relapse), gametocytocides stop transmission to mosquitoes.",["Classification by stage: blood schizonticides (chloroquine, quinine, mefloquine, artemisinins, lumefantrine, piperaquine, atovaquone, sulfadoxine–pyrimethamine); tissue schizonticides (primaquine, tafenoquine — hypnozoites); gametocytocides (primaquine — P. falciparum; chloroquine/quinine — vivax); sporontocides (proguanil, pyrimethamine).","Objectives: clinical cure, radical cure (prevent relapse — vivax/ovale), prevent transmission, prophylaxis (causal/suppressive).","Chloroquine: concentrates in parasite food vacuole, inhibits haem polymerase → toxic haem; uses — vivax/ovale/malariae, sensitive falciparum, extraintestinal amoebiasis, RA, SLE; ADR — pruritus, retinopathy (bull's eye), QT, myopathy. Primaquine: 0.25 mg/kg × 14 days (vivax radical cure), 0.75 mg/kg single (falciparum gametocytes); haemolysis in G6PD deficiency, avoid pregnancy/infants. Mefloquine: blood schizonticide, prophylaxis (weekly) — neuropsychiatric ADR.","NVBDCP: vivax — chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days + primaquine 14 days; falciparum — ACT: artesunate + SP (AS + SP) × 3 days + single primaquine (NE states: artemether–lumefantrine); severe malaria — IV artesunate then full ACT; pregnancy 1st trimester — quinine + clindamycin (now ACT acceptable)."],"Primaquine needs G6PD test; ACT in India = AS + SP (AL in North-East)."],
"Malaria is caused by Plasmodium falciparum, P. vivax, P. ovale, P. malariae and P. knowlesi, transmitted by female Anopheles mosquitoes. Life cycle and drug targets: sporozoites (from mosquito) → primary exoerythrocytic (tissue) schizogony in liver → merozoites invade RBCs → erythrocytic schizogony (clinical attacks) → some form gametocytes (transmission to mosquito); in P. vivax and P. ovale some sporozoites remain dormant in liver as hypnozoites causing relapses. Classification by stage of action: (1) causal prophylactics (primary tissue schizonticides) — primaquine, proguanil, atovaquone (prevent hepatic phase); (2) blood schizonticides (suppressive/clinical cure) — fast-acting high-efficacy: chloroquine, amodiaquine, quinine, mefloquine, halofantrine, lumefantrine, piperaquine, pyronaridine, artemisinins (artesunate, artemether, arteether, dihydroartemisinin), atovaquone; slow-acting low-efficacy: proguanil, pyrimethamine, sulfonamides, tetracyclines/doxycycline, clindamycin; (3) tissue schizonticides for radical cure (hypnozoitocides) — primaquine, tafenoquine; (4) gametocytocides — primaquine (all species including falciparum), chloroquine and quinine (vivax, malariae, ovale), artemisinins (partial); (5) sporontocides (prevent development in mosquito) — proguanil, pyrimethamine. Chemical classification: 4-aminoquinolines (chloroquine, amodiaquine, piperaquine), cinchona alkaloids (quinine, quinidine), quinoline–methanols (mefloquine), 8-aminoquinolines (primaquine, tafenoquine), biguanides (proguanil), diaminopyrimidines (pyrimethamine), sulfonamides/sulfones (sulfadoxine, dapsone), artemisinin derivatives, amino-alcohols (lumefantrine, halofantrine), naphthoquinone (atovaquone), antibiotics (doxycycline, clindamycin). Objectives of antimalarial therapy: (1) causal prophylaxis — prevent establishment of liver infection; (2) suppressive prophylaxis — suppress erythrocytic phase so clinical attack does not occur (travellers); (3) clinical cure — terminate erythrocytic schizogony and acute attack (blood schizonticides); (4) radical cure — eradicate both erythrocytic and exoerythrocytic (hypnozoite) forms to prevent relapse (vivax/ovale: blood schizonticide + primaquine); (5) gametocidal — prevent transmission (single-dose primaquine in falciparum); (6) mass/presumptive treatment in outbreaks and seasonal chemoprevention. Chloroquine: 4-aminoquinoline; mechanism — weak base concentrated (pH trapping) in the acidic food vacuole of the intraerythrocytic parasite; inhibits haem polymerase, preventing detoxification of haem (ferriprotoporphyrin IX released from digested haemoglobin) into inert haemozoin; free haem and haem–chloroquine complex are toxic to parasite membranes; resistance — PfCRT (chloroquine resistance transporter) mutations causing efflux from vacuole; widespread in P. falciparum (India); P. vivax mostly sensitive; pharmacokinetics — well absorbed, huge Vd (sequestered in tissues — liver, spleen, kidney, lung, melanin-containing tissues: retina, skin), very long terminal t½ (1–2 months), loading dose needed; uses — (a) P. vivax, P. ovale, P. malariae, P. knowlesi and chloroquine-sensitive falciparum (total 25 mg/kg base over 3 days: 10 mg/kg day 1 and 2, 5 mg/kg day 3, or 10-10-5 / 600-600-300 mg in adults); (b) chemoprophylaxis in sensitive areas (300 mg base weekly); (c) extraintestinal amoebiasis (amoebic liver abscess — with metronidazole), (d) rheumatoid arthritis, SLE, discoid lupus (hydroxychloroquine — DMARD), (e) photogenic reactions, giardiasis, lepra reactions; adverse effects — at antimalarial doses: nausea, vomiting, epigastric pain, headache, dizziness, blurred vision, pruritus (common in Africans and Indians), skin rashes; high/long-term doses: retinopathy (bull's eye maculopathy; irreversible — annual eye exam on long-term HCQ), corneal deposits, ototoxicity, myopathy and cardiomyopathy, peripheral neuropathy, bleaching of hair, psychosis, seizures, QT prolongation; IV/IM rapid injection — hypotension, cardiac arrest (especially children); acute overdose highly toxic (cardiovascular collapse — diazepam and adrenaline); haemolysis in G6PD deficiency (rare); safe in pregnancy. Mefloquine: quinoline–methanol; blood schizonticide (food vacuole/ribosome action), not active against hypnozoites or gametocytes; long t½ (2–3 weeks); uses — chemoprophylaxis for travellers to chloroquine-resistant areas (250 mg weekly starting 2–3 weeks before travel and continuing 4 weeks after), treatment of uncomplicated multidrug-resistant falciparum malaria (with artesunate — AS + MQ ACT); adverse effects — GI upset, dizziness, vivid dreams, insomnia, anxiety, depression, hallucinations, psychosis, seizures (neuropsychiatric effects may persist — avoid in history of psychiatric disorders, epilepsy), sinus bradycardia, QT prolongation (avoid with quinine/halofantrine), teratogenicity in animals (but used in 2nd/3rd trimester). Primaquine: 8-aminoquinoline; mechanism — not fully known; metabolised (CYP2D6) to oxidant quinone-imine intermediates that generate reactive oxygen species and interfere with parasite mitochondrial electron transport; active against hypnozoites and primary liver stages of all species and gametocytes of all species including P. falciparum; weak blood schizonticide; pharmacokinetics — oral, short t½ 6 h, daily dosing; uses — (a) radical cure of P. vivax and P. ovale malaria (anti-relapse) — 0.25 mg/kg/day for 14 days (NVBDCP; 15 mg adults) with chloroquine (0.5 mg/kg for 7 days in some regions); 0.75 mg/kg weekly × 8 weeks in mild G6PD deficiency; (b) gametocytocidal in P. falciparum — single dose 0.75 mg/kg (WHO now 0.25 mg/kg single low dose — safe without G6PD testing) on day 2 with ACT to reduce transmission; (c) causal prophylaxis (terminal prophylaxis after travel); (d) Pneumocystis pneumonia (with clindamycin); adverse effects — dose-related acute haemolytic anaemia in G6PD-deficient individuals (screen with G6PD test; common in India's tribal populations), methaemoglobinaemia, abdominal cramps, nausea (take with food), leucopenia, rarely hypertension and arrhythmias; contraindications — pregnancy (fetus may be G6PD deficient), infants < 6 months (NVBDCP: avoid < 1 year for 14-day course), severe G6PD deficiency, breastfeeding infants with unknown G6PD, concurrent myelosuppressive drugs, active RA/SLE (granulocytopenia); tafenoquine — single-dose radical cure (long t½), requires G6PD activity > 70%. Artemisinins: derived from Artemisia annua (Qinghaosu); endoperoxide bridge activated by haem iron generates free radicals that alkylate parasite proteins (PfATP6, others); fastest-acting blood schizonticides with broad stage specificity (including young ring forms), reduce parasite biomass 10,000-fold per cycle, partially gametocytocidal; short t½ (1 h) — must be combined with a longer-acting partner drug (ACT) to prevent recrudescence and resistance; artesunate (oral, IV, IM, rectal), artemether (oral, IM), arteether (IM), dihydroartemisinin; partial resistance (kelch13 mutations) in Greater Mekong, emerging in Africa and NE India; ADR — generally well tolerated; delayed haemolysis after IV artesunate, neutropenia, QT (partners). ACT regimens: artesunate + sulfadoxine–pyrimethamine (AS + SP; India except NE), artemether–lumefantrine (AL; NE states and where SP resistance), artesunate + mefloquine, artesunate + amodiaquine, dihydroartemisinin + piperaquine, artesunate + pyronaridine, arterolane–piperaquine (India). National guidelines (NVBDCP/NCVBDC 2013/2023): diagnosis by microscopy or bivalent RDT before treatment; (1) P. vivax — chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days + primaquine 0.25 mg/kg daily × 14 days (under supervision; not for pregnant women, infants, G6PD deficiency); (2) P. falciparum uncomplicated — ACT for 3 days: artesunate 4 mg/kg daily × 3 days + SP (sulfadoxine 25 mg/kg + pyrimethamine 1.25 mg/kg) single dose on day 1 (AS + SP), except in North-Eastern states where artemether–lumefantrine (20/120 mg, weight-based twice daily × 3 days) is used due to SP resistance; plus single-dose primaquine 0.75 mg/kg on day 2 (gametocytocidal; avoid in pregnancy and infants < 1 year); (3) mixed infection (Pv + Pf) — ACT for 3 days + primaquine 14 days; (4) pregnancy — first trimester: quinine 10 mg/kg TDS × 7 days (with clindamycin) for falciparum (WHO now permits AL in first trimester); second and third trimesters: ACT (AS + SP / AL); vivax in pregnancy — chloroquine (primaquine deferred until after delivery and breastfeeding); (5) severe/complicated malaria (any species; cerebral malaria, severe anaemia, hypoglycaemia, ARDS, AKI, shock, jaundice with parasitaemia, hyperparasitaemia) — parenteral artesunate 2.4 mg/kg IV/IM at 0, 12, 24 h then daily (preferred, including pregnancy all trimesters), alternatives — artemether IM, arteether IM, quinine IV (20 mg/kg loading over 4 h then 10 mg/kg 8-hourly; with glucose monitoring — hypoglycaemia, cinchonism, QT); switch to full oral ACT course once patient can swallow (after at least 24 h of parenteral therapy); supportive — fluids, glucose, antipyretics, anticonvulsants, transfusion, dialysis; rectal artesunate pre-referral in children; (6) chemoprophylaxis for travellers/high-risk groups — doxycycline 100 mg daily (from 2 days before to 4 weeks after; not in pregnancy/children < 8), mefloquine 250 mg weekly, atovaquone–proguanil daily, chloroquine weekly (vivax-only areas).",
[["Classification by stage",[["Stage","Drugs"],["Causal prophylactics (liver)","Primaquine, proguanil, atovaquone"],["Blood schizonticides (fast)","Chloroquine, quinine, mefloquine, artemisinins, lumefantrine, piperaquine, atovaquone"],["Blood schizonticides (slow)","Pyrimethamine, sulfadoxine, doxycycline, clindamycin, proguanil"],["Hypnozoitocides (radical cure)","Primaquine, tafenoquine"],["Gametocytocides","Primaquine (all); chloroquine, quinine (vivax)"],["Sporontocides","Proguanil, pyrimethamine"]]],
["Key drugs",[["Drug","MOA","Use","Key ADR"],["Chloroquine","Inhibits haem polymerase","Vivax, sensitive falciparum; RA, SLE; amoebic liver abscess","Pruritus, retinopathy, QT, myopathy"],["Mefloquine","Blood schizonticide","Prophylaxis; MDR falciparum (with artesunate)","Neuropsychiatric, bradycardia"],["Primaquine","Oxidative stress (8-aminoquinoline)","Radical cure (vivax); falciparum gametocytes","Haemolysis (G6PD), methaemoglobinaemia"],["Artesunate","Endoperoxide free radicals","ACT; severe malaria (IV)","Delayed haemolysis"],["Quinine","Haem polymerase","Severe malaria, 1st trimester","Cinchonism, hypoglycaemia, QT"]]],
["NVBDCP treatment",[["Situation","Regimen"],["P. vivax","Chloroquine 25 mg/kg × 3 d + primaquine 0.25 mg/kg × 14 d"],["P. falciparum (uncomplicated)","AS + SP × 3 d (AL in North-East) + primaquine 0.75 mg/kg single dose day 2"],["Mixed","ACT + primaquine 14 d"],["Pregnancy 1st trimester (Pf)","Quinine + clindamycin (AL acceptable per WHO)"],["Pregnancy 2nd/3rd trimester","ACT"],["Severe malaria","IV/IM artesunate → full oral ACT"],["Prophylaxis","Doxycycline daily / mefloquine weekly"]]],
["Objectives of therapy",["Clinical cure (blood schizonticides)","Radical cure (+ primaquine for vivax/ovale)","Prevent transmission (gametocytocides)","Causal and suppressive prophylaxis","Prevent resistance (combination — ACT)"]]],
{i:"Antimalarial therapy is guided by species, severity, drug resistance and patient factors; ACTs and primaquine radical cure are central to India's malaria elimination goals.",x:[["Primaquine precautions",["Test G6PD before 14-day course","Avoid in pregnancy, infants < 1 year, breastfeeding (unknown infant G6PD)","Take with food (GI upset)","Watch for dark urine (haemolysis)"]]],f:"Mosquito bite → sporozoites → liver schizont (← primaquine, proguanil, atovaquone) / hypnozoites (← primaquine, tafenoquine)\n → merozoites → RBC schizogony = fever (← chloroquine, ACT, quinine, mefloquine)\n → gametocytes (← primaquine) → mosquito (← proguanil, pyrimethamine)",c:"Chloroquine treats vivax malaria, primaquine provides radical cure and blocks falciparum transmission, mefloquine serves prophylaxis and resistant malaria, and artemisinin-based combinations are first-line for falciparum and severe malaria under national guidelines."});

Z("pharm11",4,"ph",["Metronidazole is a nitroimidazole that kills anaerobic bacteria and protozoa by damaging their DNA; it is the drug of choice for amoebiasis (intestinal and liver abscess), giardiasis, trichomoniasis and anaerobic infections.","Only anaerobic organisms have the machinery to 'switch on' metronidazole (reduce its nitro group), so it becomes a toxic DNA-breaker inside them while sparing us.",["MOA: nitro group reduced by ferredoxin/PFOR in anaerobes → reactive intermediates → DNA strand breaks → cidal.","Uses: amoebiasis — intestinal (800 mg TDS × 5–10 days) and amoebic liver abscess, followed by luminal amoebicide (diloxanide furoate, paromomycin); giardiasis; trichomoniasis (2 g single dose; treat partner); bacterial vaginosis; anaerobic infections (B. fragilis, intra-abdominal, pelvic, brain abscess); C. difficile (mild, now second-line); H. pylori; surgical prophylaxis; dental infections; tetanus.","ADR: metallic taste, nausea, furred tongue, dark urine, disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol, peripheral neuropathy, seizures, encephalopathy (cerebellar) with prolonged use; potentiates warfarin, lithium.","Related: tinidazole, secnidazole, ornidazole (longer t½; single dose); luminal agents for cyst passers."],"Metronidazole + alcohol = disulfiram reaction."],
"Amoebiasis, caused by Entamoeba histolytica, may be intestinal (asymptomatic cyst passage, non-dysenteric colitis, amoebic dysentery) or extraintestinal (amoebic liver abscess, lung, brain). Classification of antiamoebic drugs: (1) tissue amoebicides — for both intestinal wall and extraintestinal disease: nitroimidazoles (metronidazole, tinidazole, secnidazole, ornidazole, satranidazole), alkaloids (emetine, dehydroemetine — obsolete); for extraintestinal only: chloroquine (liver); (2) luminal amoebicides — act in the gut lumen on trophozoites and cysts: amide (diloxanide furoate), 8-hydroxyquinolines (quiniodochlor, iodoquinol), antibiotics (paromomycin, tetracyclines — indirect, nitazoxanide). Metronidazole: 5-nitroimidazole; mechanism — enters cells by diffusion; in anaerobic organisms (bacteria and protozoa), the nitro group is reduced by electron transport proteins (ferredoxin, pyruvate:ferredoxin oxidoreductase) to short-lived nitroso free radicals and hydroxylamine intermediates that damage DNA (strand breaks, helix destabilisation) and other macromolecules — cidal; aerobic cells lack the low-redox potential pathway, conferring selectivity; resistance rare (reduced activation, efflux). Spectrum: protozoa — Entamoeba histolytica, Giardia lamblia, Trichomonas vaginalis, Balantidium coli, Blastocystis; anaerobic bacteria — Bacteroides fragilis, Prevotella, Fusobacterium, Clostridium (including C. difficile and C. tetani), Peptostreptococcus, Gardnerella vaginalis (bacterial vaginosis), Helicobacter pylori (microaerophilic; resistance common), Campylobacter; not aerobes or facultative anaerobes, Actinomyces, Propionibacterium. Pharmacokinetics: almost complete oral absorption (> 90%), also IV, rectal, vaginal, topical; widely distributed into all tissues including CSF, brain abscess, bone, vaginal secretions, bile, saliva, breast milk, placenta; metabolised in liver (hydroxylation, active metabolite), t½ 8 h; urine may be dark/red-brown. Uses: (1) amoebiasis — intestinal amoebiasis/amoebic dysentery: 400–800 mg TDS for 5–10 days (children 30–50 mg/kg/day); amoebic liver abscess: 750–800 mg TDS for 7–10 days (drug of choice; aspiration if large/left lobe/no response) — must be followed by a luminal amoebicide (diloxanide furoate 500 mg TDS × 10 days or paromomycin) to eradicate intestinal cysts because metronidazole is well absorbed and leaves little in the colon; asymptomatic cyst passers — luminal agent alone; (2) giardiasis — 250–400 mg TDS × 5–7 days (or tinidazole 2 g single dose); (3) trichomoniasis — 2 g single dose or 500 mg BD × 7 days; treat sexual partner simultaneously; (4) bacterial vaginosis — 500 mg BD × 7 days or 0.75% vaginal gel; (5) anaerobic and mixed bacterial infections — intra-abdominal (peritonitis, appendicitis, abscess) and pelvic (PID, puerperal sepsis, post-abortal) infections with aerobic Gram-negative cover, brain abscess, lung abscess/aspiration pneumonia, osteomyelitis, endocarditis due to B. fragilis, diabetic foot, necrotising infections; (6) Clostridioides difficile colitis — mild–moderate (oral; now oral vancomycin/fidaxomicin preferred); (7) H. pylori eradication (with PPI and clarithromycin/amoxicillin or in bismuth quadruple); (8) surgical prophylaxis in colorectal, gynaecological and appendix surgery; (9) dental infections — acute ulcerative gingivitis (Vincent's), periodontitis, dental abscess; (10) tetanus (preferred to penicillin), Crohn disease perianal fistulae, pouchitis, rosacea (topical), Dracunculus (adjunct), balantidiasis. Adverse effects: common — metallic/bitter taste, anorexia, nausea, vomiting, abdominal cramps, furred tongue, glossitis, stomatitis, dry mouth, diarrhoea; headache, dizziness, dark urine; CNS — peripheral sensory neuropathy (prolonged use), vertigo, ataxia, seizures, encephalopathy and cerebellar toxicity (high cumulative doses — MRI dentate nucleus changes); aseptic meningitis; optic neuropathy; rashes, urticaria, pruritus; transient leucopenia, neutropenia; candidiasis; disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol (inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase — flushing, vomiting, headache, palpitations — avoid alcohol during and for 48 h after; 72 h after tinidazole); mutagenic in bacteria and carcinogenic in rodents (no human evidence) — avoid in first trimester of pregnancy if possible (single-dose regimens avoided; now considered acceptable when indicated), avoid high-dose in lactation. Interactions: potentiates warfarin (CYP2C9 inhibition — bleeding), raises lithium, phenytoin, busulfan, 5-FU, cyclosporine levels; disulfiram (psychosis — avoid within 2 weeks); enzyme inducers (phenobarbitone, rifampicin) reduce efficacy; cimetidine raises levels. Tinidazole, secnidazole and ornidazole have longer half-lives allowing single-dose or shorter regimens with similar efficacy and fewer GI effects.",
[["Antiamoebic drugs",[["Group","Drugs","Site"],["Tissue amoebicides (intestinal + extraintestinal)","Metronidazole, tinidazole, secnidazole, ornidazole","Intestinal wall, liver"],["Extraintestinal only","Chloroquine","Liver"],["Luminal amoebicides","Diloxanide furoate, paromomycin, quiniodochlor, nitazoxanide","Gut lumen (cysts)"]]],
["Uses of metronidazole",[["Indication","Regimen"],["Amoebic dysentery","400–800 mg TDS × 5–10 d + luminal agent"],["Amoebic liver abscess","750–800 mg TDS × 7–10 d + luminal agent"],["Giardiasis","250–400 mg TDS × 5–7 d"],["Trichomoniasis","2 g single dose (treat partner)"],["Bacterial vaginosis","500 mg BD × 7 d"],["Anaerobic infections","With aerobic cover"],["H. pylori, C. difficile, surgical prophylaxis, tetanus","—"]]],
["Adverse effects",["Metallic taste, nausea, furred tongue","Dark urine","Disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol","Peripheral neuropathy, seizures, cerebellar toxicity","Leucopenia","Warfarin, lithium interactions"]]],
{c:"Metronidazole is the cornerstone tissue amoebicide and anti-anaerobic agent; it must be followed by a luminal amoebicide in amoebiasis, and patients must avoid alcohol during therapy."});

Z("pharm11",5,"ph",["Anthelmintics expel or kill parasitic worms; albendazole and mebendazole (benzimidazoles) are broad-spectrum drugs for intestinal worms, diethylcarbamazine (DEC) and ivermectin treat filariasis, and praziquantel treats tapeworms and flukes.","Benzimidazoles starve worms by blocking their microtubules (glucose uptake); ivermectin and praziquantel paralyse worms so they are expelled; DEC unmasks microfilariae for the immune system to destroy.",["Albendazole/mebendazole: bind β-tubulin → ↓ microtubules → ↓ glucose uptake → worm death; albendazole 400 mg single dose (roundworm, hookworm, whipworm, pinworm — repeat 2 weeks), 3 days (Strongyloides, Trichuris heavy), neurocysticercosis (15 mg/kg/day × 8–28 days with steroids), hydatid (cycles), NDD (biannual school deworming); ADR — GI upset, hepatitis, alopecia, marrow suppression (long), teratogenic (avoid 1st trimester).","DEC: microfilaricide (and some macrofilaricide) for lymphatic filariasis (Wuchereria, Brugia) — 6 mg/kg × 12 days; MDA (DEC + albendazole ± ivermectin — IDA) annually; tropical pulmonary eosinophilia; loiasis; Mazzotti reaction (fever, rash, itching due to dying microfilariae).","Ivermectin: activates glutamate-gated Cl⁻ channels → paralysis of nematodes; drug of choice for strongyloidiasis, onchocerciasis, scabies (crusted), head lice; MDA for filariasis; 200 µg/kg; CI pregnancy, children < 15 kg.","Praziquantel: ↑ Ca²⁺ permeability → tetanic contraction + tegument damage; schistosomiasis, tapeworms (Taenia 10 mg/kg single), neurocysticercosis, liver/lung flukes (not Fasciola — triclabendazole); others — pyrantel pamoate (roundworm, hookworm, pinworm), niclosamide (tapeworm)."],"National Deworming Day: albendazole 400 mg (200 mg 1–2 yr) twice yearly."],
"Helminthiasis is highly prevalent in India (soil-transmitted helminths affecting children, filariasis, neurocysticercosis). Classification of anthelmintics by worm type: (1) nematodes (roundworms) — intestinal: Ascaris (roundworm), Ancylostoma/Necator (hookworm), Enterobius (pinworm), Trichuris (whipworm), Strongyloides (threadworm) — albendazole, mebendazole, pyrantel pamoate, ivermectin, levamisole, piperazine; tissue: filariae (Wuchereria bancrofti, Brugia malayi, Onchocerca, Loa loa) — diethylcarbamazine, ivermectin, albendazole, doxycycline (Wolbachia); Trichinella — albendazole, mebendazole; larva migrans — albendazole, ivermectin; Dracunculus — metronidazole/extraction; (2) cestodes (tapeworms) — Taenia solium and saginata, Hymenolepis nana, Diphyllobothrium — praziquantel, niclosamide, albendazole; larval cestodes: neurocysticercosis — albendazole ± praziquantel; hydatid disease (Echinococcus) — albendazole (with surgery/PAIR); (3) trematodes (flukes) — Schistosoma, Clonorchis, Opisthorchis, Paragonimus — praziquantel; Fasciola hepatica — triclabendazole. Benzimidazoles (albendazole, mebendazole, thiabendazole, flubendazole, triclabendazole): mechanism — bind selectively to parasite β-tubulin with higher affinity than mammalian tubulin, inhibiting polymerisation into microtubules; this impairs glucose uptake, depletes glycogen and ATP, and disrupts cell division and egg production — slow immobilisation and death of adult worms; also larvicidal and ovicidal. Albendazole: poorly absorbed (absorption increased 5-fold by fatty meal — for tissue infections; empty stomach for intestinal), converted in liver to active albendazole sulfoxide (enters CSF, hydatid cysts), t½ 8–12 h; broadest spectrum — Ascaris, hookworm, Trichuris, Enterobius, Strongyloides, Trichinella, larva migrans (cutaneous and visceral), Taenia, Hymenolepis, neurocysticercosis, hydatid cyst, Clonorchis, filariae (adjunct in MDA), Giardia; uses and doses — single 400 mg dose (200 mg in children 1–2 years) for ascariasis, hookworm, trichuriasis (3 days for heavy), enterobiasis (repeat after 2 weeks; treat family); strongyloidiasis — 400 mg BD × 3–7 days (ivermectin preferred); neurocysticercosis — 15 mg/kg/day in two doses for 8–30 days with corticosteroids (dexamethasone/prednisolone to reduce inflammation from dying cysts) and antiepileptics; combination with praziquantel for multiple cysts; hydatid disease — 400 mg BD in 28-day cycles with 14-day gaps (3–6 cycles) before/after surgery or PAIR, or alone for inoperable cysts; cutaneous larva migrans 3 days; trichinosis; lymphatic filariasis mass drug administration (with DEC/ivermectin); National Deworming Day (NDD) — albendazole 400 mg chewable twice yearly to all children 1–19 years in schools and anganwadis; adverse effects — single-dose: mild epigastric pain, nausea, diarrhoea, headache, dizziness; prolonged high-dose: raised transaminases, hepatitis, alopecia, leucopenia/pancytopenia (monitor blood counts and LFT), fever; neurological reactions (seizures, raised ICP) in neurocysticercosis from inflammatory response; teratogenic and embryotoxic in animals — avoid in first trimester of pregnancy (single dose permitted in second/third trimester in deworming programmes), children < 1 year; interactions — dexamethasone, praziquantel, cimetidine increase albendazole sulfoxide levels; carbamazepine, phenytoin reduce. Mebendazole: very poorly absorbed (< 10%; acts in gut lumen) — 100 mg BD × 3 days or 500 mg single dose for intestinal nematodes; 100 mg single for pinworm; less effective for tissue infections; similar adverse effects. Diethylcarbamazine (DEC): piperazine derivative; mechanism — rapidly reduces circulating microfilariae by altering their surface membranes (unmasking antigens), making them susceptible to phagocytosis by host immune cells; inhibits arachidonic acid metabolism in microfilariae and endothelium; hyperpolarisation causes paralysis; slowly kills adult worms (macrofilaricidal partially); pharmacokinetics — well absorbed, t½ 8 h (prolonged in alkaline urine); uses — lymphatic filariasis (W. bancrofti, B. malayi) — 6 mg/kg/day in 3 divided doses for 12 days (repeat courses); mass drug administration (MDA) under National Programme for Elimination of Lymphatic Filariasis — annual single dose DEC 6 mg/kg + albendazole 400 mg (DA), or triple drug IDA (ivermectin + DEC + albendazole) in high-burden districts; tropical pulmonary eosinophilia (drug of choice; 3 weeks); loiasis (drug of choice; risk of encephalopathy with high microfilaraemia); Toxocara (visceral larva migrans); onchocerciasis — avoided (severe Mazzotti reaction, eye damage); adverse effects — drug itself: anorexia, nausea, headache, dizziness, drowsiness; reactions due to dying microfilariae (Mazzotti reaction): fever, malaise, headache, myalgia, arthralgia, pruritus, rash, lymphadenitis, lymphangitis, hypotension, wheezing; worse with heavy infection — start with low dose, antihistamines/steroids; contraindicated in pregnancy (MDA excludes pregnant women, children < 2 years, severely ill). Ivermectin: macrocyclic lactone (avermectin from Streptomyces avermitilis); mechanism — binds glutamate-gated chloride channels (and GABA-gated) in invertebrate nerve and muscle cells, increasing Cl⁻ permeability, hyperpolarisation and paralysis of pharyngeal and somatic muscles; does not cross mammalian BBB (P-gp; GABA receptors protected); microfilaricidal (sustained reduction), sterilises adult female worms; uses — strongyloidiasis (drug of choice; 200 µg/kg single dose or for 2 days; repeated in hyperinfection), onchocerciasis (drug of choice; annual/biannual mass treatment — Mectizan donation), lymphatic filariasis MDA (IDA), cutaneous larva migrans, ascariasis, trichuriasis (partial), scabies (oral 200 µg/kg repeated after 7–14 days; crusted/Norwegian scabies with topical permethrin; institutional outbreaks), head lice (oral/topical), rosacea (topical 1%); adverse effects — generally mild: fever, pruritus, rash, dizziness, headache, myalgia, lymph node tenderness (Mazzotti-like reaction in onchocerciasis), hypotension; encephalopathy in patients with heavy Loa loa microfilaraemia (> 30,000/mL); avoid in pregnancy, lactation (first week), children < 15 kg or < 5 years, CNS disorders with BBB disruption. Praziquantel: pyrazinoisoquinoline; mechanism — increases permeability of the parasite tegument to Ca²⁺ (interacts with TRPM channels, β subunits of voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels), causing massive Ca²⁺ influx, spastic paralysis and contraction of worm musculature; vacuolisation and disintegration of the tegument exposing antigens to host immune attack; dislodges worms from tissues; pharmacokinetics — well absorbed (enhanced with food), extensive first-pass metabolism (CYP3A4 — levels reduced by rifampicin, carbamazepine, phenytoin, dexamethasone; raised by cimetidine), enters CSF, t½ 1–1.5 h; uses — schistosomiasis (all species; drug of choice; 40–60 mg/kg single dose or divided), Taenia solium and saginata intestinal infection (5–10 mg/kg single dose), Hymenolepis nana (25 mg/kg single dose, repeat), Diphyllobothrium latum, neurocysticercosis (50 mg/kg/day for 15 days; with albendazole for multiple cysts; plus steroids), liver flukes — Clonorchis, Opisthorchis (25 mg/kg TDS × 1–2 days), lung fluke Paragonimus (25 mg/kg TDS × 2 days), intestinal flukes (Fasciolopsis buski); not effective against Fasciola hepatica (triclabendazole) or hydatid cyst (limited; adjunct); adverse effects — headache, dizziness, drowsiness, malaise, abdominal discomfort, nausea, fever, urticaria (from dying worms); neurological worsening in neurocysticercosis (steroids); contraindicated in ocular cysticercosis (inflammation destroys the eye); cautious in pregnancy (considered safe in programmes) — avoid breastfeeding for 72 h. Other anthelmintics: pyrantel pamoate (depolarising neuromuscular blocker in worms — spastic paralysis; roundworm, hookworm, pinworm; safe in pregnancy and children; antagonises piperazine), piperazine (GABA agonist — flaccid paralysis; ascariasis), levamisole (nicotinic agonist; also immunomodulator), niclosamide (inhibits mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation in tapeworms; Taenia, Hymenolepis; scolex digested — risk of cysticercosis with T. solium, give purgative), triclabendazole (fascioliasis), oxantel (whipworm), nitazoxanide (broad).",
[["Drug of choice by worm",[["Worm","Drug"],["Roundworm, hookworm, whipworm","Albendazole / mebendazole"],["Pinworm","Albendazole/mebendazole/pyrantel (repeat 2 wk; treat family)"],["Strongyloides","Ivermectin"],["Lymphatic filariasis","DEC + albendazole (± ivermectin — IDA)"],["Onchocerciasis","Ivermectin"],["Tapeworm (intestinal)","Praziquantel / niclosamide"],["Neurocysticercosis","Albendazole ± praziquantel + steroids"],["Hydatid cyst","Albendazole + surgery/PAIR"],["Schistosomiasis, liver/lung flukes","Praziquantel"],["Fasciola","Triclabendazole"],["Scabies (crusted), head lice","Ivermectin"]]],
["Mechanisms",[["Drug","Mechanism","Effect"],["Albendazole/mebendazole","Bind β-tubulin","↓ Glucose uptake, death"],["DEC","Unmasks microfilariae; immune clearance","Microfilaricidal"],["Ivermectin","Glutamate-gated Cl⁻ channels","Paralysis"],["Praziquantel","↑ Ca²⁺ influx, tegument damage","Spastic paralysis"],["Pyrantel","Depolarising NM block","Spastic paralysis"],["Piperazine","GABA agonist","Flaccid paralysis"],["Niclosamide","Uncouples oxidative phosphorylation","Tapeworm death"]]],
["Adverse effects / precautions",["Albendazole: hepatitis, marrow suppression (prolonged), teratogenic","DEC: Mazzotti reaction; avoid in onchocerciasis, pregnancy","Ivermectin: Loa loa encephalopathy; avoid < 15 kg, pregnancy","Praziquantel: CI in ocular cysticercosis; steroids in NCC","Niclosamide: risk of cysticercosis with T. solium"]]],
{i:"India runs National Deworming Day and the lymphatic filariasis elimination programme using albendazole, DEC and ivermectin in mass drug administration.",f:"Benzimidazoles → β-tubulin → ↓ microtubules → ↓ glucose → worm death\n DEC → microfilarial surface change → phagocytosis\n Ivermectin → GluCl channels → Cl⁻ influx → paralysis\n Praziquantel → Ca²⁺ influx → contraction + tegument damage",c:"Albendazole is the broad-spectrum mainstay for intestinal and larval helminths, DEC and ivermectin drive filariasis elimination, and praziquantel is the drug of choice for tapeworms and flukes."});

Z("pharm11",6,"ph",["Leprosy is treated with multidrug therapy (MDT) — rifampicin, dapsone and clofazimine — to cure infection and prevent resistance; paucibacillary leprosy is treated for 6 months and multibacillary for 12 months.","M. leprae grows very slowly, so drugs must be continued for months; three drugs together make sure no resistant bacilli survive.",["Dapsone: sulfone, inhibits folate synthesis (DHPS) — bacteriostatic; ADR — haemolysis (G6PD), methaemoglobinaemia, dapsone hypersensitivity syndrome (fever, rash, hepatitis, lymphadenopathy), agranulocytosis, neuropathy.","Rifampicin: rapidly bactericidal; 600 mg once monthly supervised; ADR — hepatitis, orange urine, flu-like syndrome (intermittent).","Clofazimine: riminophenazine; binds DNA, weakly bactericidal, anti-inflammatory (ENL); ADR — reddish-brown skin pigmentation, ichthyosis, GI (crystal enteropathy).","WHO/NLEP 2018 uniform MDT (all patients): rifampicin 600 mg monthly + clofazimine 300 mg monthly (supervised) + clofazimine 50 mg daily + dapsone 100 mg daily — PB 6 months, MB 12 months; children reduced doses. Reactions: type 1 (reversal) — prednisolone; type 2 (ENL) — prednisolone, clofazimine, thalidomide; MDT continued. PEP: single-dose rifampicin to contacts."],"Leprosy MDT: 'R monthly, C monthly + daily, D daily'; PB 6, MB 12 months."],
"Leprosy (Hansen disease) is a chronic granulomatous infection caused by Mycobacterium leprae affecting skin and peripheral nerves; India accounts for over half of new global cases. Classification for treatment (WHO): paucibacillary (PB) — 1–5 skin lesions, no nerve involvement or one nerve, slit-skin smear negative; multibacillary (MB) — > 5 lesions, more than one nerve involved, or any smear positive. Antileprosy drugs: (1) sulfone — dapsone (DDS); (2) phenazine derivative — clofazimine; (3) antitubercular — rifampicin (rifapentine); (4) other antibiotics — ofloxacin, levofloxacin, moxifloxacin, minocycline, clarithromycin (alternatives for rifampicin resistance, intolerance; second-line); (5) for lepra reactions — corticosteroids, thalidomide, clofazimine, NSAIDs, chloroquine. Dapsone (diaminodiphenyl sulfone): mechanism — PABA analogue; inhibits dihydropteroate synthase in M. leprae (folate synthesis) — bacteriostatic; also anti-inflammatory (inhibits neutrophil myeloperoxidase); pharmacokinetics — well absorbed orally, widely distributed (skin, muscle, liver, kidney), enterohepatic circulation, acetylated (genetic polymorphism), long t½ (24–48 h); dose 100 mg daily (1–2 mg/kg children); uses — leprosy (MDT), dermatitis herpetiformis (drug of choice), Pneumocystis prophylaxis/treatment (with trimethoprim), toxoplasmosis prophylaxis (with pyrimethamine), bullous pemphigoid, linear IgA dermatosis, Sweet syndrome, pyoderma gangrenosum, brown recluse spider bite, malaria (with pyrimethamine); adverse effects — dose-related haemolytic anaemia (especially G6PD deficiency), methaemoglobinaemia, GI intolerance, headache, peripheral neuropathy (motor), hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice, agranulocytosis (first months), exfoliative dermatitis, fixed drug eruption, dapsone hypersensitivity syndrome ('sulfone syndrome' — fever, rash, lymphadenopathy, hepatitis, eosinophilia, 4–6 weeks after starting; HLA-B*13:01; potentially fatal — stop and give steroids), psychosis, lepra reaction (type 1) precipitation. Rifampicin: most potent bactericidal drug against M. leprae — a single 600 mg dose kills 99.9% of viable bacilli within days; given once monthly under supervision in MDT (intermittent monthly dosing is effective due to slow multiplication and minimises adverse effects/cost); adverse effects at monthly dosing are few (hepatitis, flu-like syndrome, thrombocytopenia, renal failure rare); orange urine. Clofazimine: riminophenazine dye; binds preferentially to mycobacterial DNA (guanine-rich), inhibits template function; generates reactive oxygen species; membrane destabilisation; weakly bactericidal against M. leprae; also anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive (useful in erythema nodosum leprosum, ENL); pharmacokinetics — variable absorption (with food), highly lipophilic, accumulates in fat, reticuloendothelial system, skin and macrophages, very long t½ (~70 days); uses — MB leprosy (and since 2018 PB) in MDT, chronic/recurrent ENL (high doses 300 mg/day for up to 3 months), MDR-TB (group B), MAC (alternative), discoid lupus, pyoderma gangrenosum; adverse effects — reddish to brownish-black skin discolouration (especially lesions; reversible over months to years after stopping; distressing), discoloured urine, sweat, tears, conjunctiva, dryness of skin and ichthyosis, photosensitivity, GI symptoms — abdominal pain, diarrhoea, weight loss; crystal deposition in intestinal mucosa causing enteropathy and subacute intestinal obstruction (with high doses), splenic infarction, QT prolongation (with bedaquiline), depression. Multidrug therapy (MDT) — rationale: prevent emergence of drug resistance (dapsone monotherapy led to widespread resistance), rapidly reduce infectiousness, shorten duration, reduce relapse. WHO 2018 and NLEP guidelines (uniform three-drug regimen for PB and MB): adults (> 15 years, > 40 kg) — rifampicin 600 mg once monthly (supervised), clofazimine 300 mg once monthly (supervised) + 50 mg daily (self-administered), dapsone 100 mg daily (self-administered); PB — 6 monthly blister packs completed within 9 months; MB — 12 monthly blister packs completed within 18 months; children 10–14 years — rifampicin 450 mg monthly, clofazimine 150 mg monthly + 50 mg alternate days, dapsone 50 mg daily; < 10 years — weight-based (rifampicin 10 mg/kg monthly, clofazimine 6 mg/kg monthly + 1 mg/kg alternate days, dapsone 2 mg/kg daily). MDT is free in India (NLEP) and accompanied by disability prevention, self-care and rehabilitation; 'release from treatment' on completion (no need for smear negativity). Rifampicin-resistant leprosy — 24 months of at least two second-line drugs (clarithromycin, minocycline or a quinolone) + clofazimine for 6 months, then clofazimine + one second-line drug for 18 months; ofloxacin-resistant — 6 months of rifampicin-free combinations. Lepra reactions (do not stop MDT): type 1 (reversal reaction; cell-mediated, borderline cases) — inflammation of existing lesions and neuritis; treatment — oral prednisolone 40–60 mg/day tapered over 12–20 weeks, NSAIDs for mild, rest and splintage of nerves; type 2 (erythema nodosum leprosum; immune complex, LL/BL) — crops of painful nodules, fever, neuritis, iritis, orchitis, arthritis; treatment — prednisolone (short course), clofazimine (high dose for steroid-dependent), thalidomide (most effective; inhibits TNF-α; teratogenic — strictly avoided in women of childbearing potential), NSAIDs, chloroquine; Lucio phenomenon. Post-exposure prophylaxis: single-dose rifampicin (SDR-PEP; 600 mg adults, 10 mg/kg children) to household and close contacts of new cases (part of NLEP — Leprosy Case Detection Campaigns) reduces risk of leprosy by ~60%.",
[["MDT regimen (adults)",[["Drug","Dose","Supervision"],["Rifampicin","600 mg once monthly","Supervised"],["Clofazimine","300 mg once monthly + 50 mg daily","Monthly supervised; daily self"],["Dapsone","100 mg daily","Self"],["Duration","PB: 6 months (within 9); MB: 12 months (within 18)","—"]]],
["Antileprosy drugs",[["Drug","MOA","Key ADR"],["Dapsone","DHPS inhibition (folate)","Haemolysis (G6PD), methaemoglobinaemia, DHS"],["Rifampicin","RNA polymerase (rapidly cidal)","Hepatitis, orange urine, flu-like"],["Clofazimine","DNA binding, anti-inflammatory","Skin pigmentation, ichthyosis, enteropathy"],["Second-line","Ofloxacin, minocycline, clarithromycin","Rifampicin resistance"]]],
["Lepra reactions",[["Reaction","Mechanism","Treatment"],["Type 1 (reversal)","Cell-mediated (borderline)","Prednisolone; continue MDT"],["Type 2 (ENL)","Immune complex (LL/BL)","Prednisolone, clofazimine, thalidomide; continue MDT"]]]],
{i:"Free MDT under NLEP has dramatically reduced leprosy prevalence; early detection, contact prophylaxis and disability prevention complete the strategy.",f:"M. leprae (slow-growing)\n Rifampicin monthly → rapid kill (99.9%)\n Dapsone daily → folate block (static)\n Clofazimine → DNA binding + anti-inflammatory\n Combination → prevents resistance; PB 6 mo, MB 12 mo",c:"Uniform three-drug MDT with rifampicin, clofazimine and dapsone cures leprosy and prevents resistance; reactions are managed with steroids or thalidomide while continuing MDT, and single-dose rifampicin protects contacts."});

Z("pharm11",7,"ph",["Sexually transmitted infections (STIs) in India are managed by the syndromic approach using colour-coded pre-packed kits (Kit 1–7) under NACO, treating all likely causes of a syndrome at the first visit without waiting for laboratory tests.","Instead of waiting for lab results, the patient's syndrome (e.g., urethral discharge) is matched to the common organisms, and a single kit covers them all — treat at first visit, treat partners.",["Kit 1 (grey) — urethral/cervical discharge, anorectal: azithromycin 1 g + cefixime 400 mg (gonorrhoea + chlamydia).","Kit 2 (green) — vaginal discharge (vaginitis): secnidazole 2 g + fluconazole 150 mg (trichomonas, BV, candida).","Kit 3 (white) — genital ulcer non-herpetic: benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU + azithromycin 1 g (syphilis, chancroid); Kit 4 (blue) — penicillin allergy: doxycycline 100 mg BD × 15 days + azithromycin.","Kit 5 (red) — herpetic ulcer: acyclovir 400 mg TDS × 7 days; Kit 6 (yellow) — lower abdominal pain (PID): cefixime + metronidazole + doxycycline × 14 days; Kit 7 (black) — inguinal bubo: doxycycline × 21 days + azithromycin. Also counselling, condoms, partner treatment, HIV/syphilis testing."],"STI kits: 1 grey (urethral), 2 green (vaginal), 3 white (ulcer), 5 red (herpes), 6 yellow (PID), 7 black (bubo)."],
"Sexually transmitted infections (gonorrhoea, chlamydia, syphilis, chancroid, herpes, trichomoniasis, bacterial vaginosis, candidiasis, LGV, donovanosis, HPV, HIV) are an important public health problem and increase HIV transmission. Syndromic case management (WHO/NACO): STIs are classified into syndromes based on symptoms and signs; treatment covers the most common organisms causing that syndrome at the first visit, using flowcharts — advantages: immediate treatment, no laboratory needed, cost-effective, reduces loss to follow-up, cuts transmission chain; disadvantages: overtreatment, misses asymptomatic infections (especially in women), resistance concerns. Components (5 Cs + 2): correct diagnosis and treatment, counselling (risk reduction, compliance), condom promotion and provision, contact (partner) notification and treatment, compliance, plus HIV and syphilis testing (RPR/VDRL) and referral to ICTC. Pre-packed colour-coded kits supplied free under NACO (Suraksha clinics): Kit 1 (grey) — urethral discharge (men), cervicitis, anorectal discharge: tablet azithromycin 1 g single dose + tablet cefixime 400 mg single dose (covers N. gonorrhoeae and C. trachomatis); Kit 2 (green) — vaginal discharge (vaginitis): tablet secnidazole 2 g single dose + tablet fluconazole 150 mg single dose (Trichomonas vaginalis, bacterial vaginosis, Candida); Kit 3 (white) — genital ulcer disease, non-herpetic: injection benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM (after test dose) + tablet azithromycin 1 g single dose (Treponema pallidum — syphilis; Haemophilus ducreyi — chancroid); Kit 4 (blue) — genital ulcer disease, non-herpetic, in penicillin-allergic patients: capsule doxycycline 100 mg BD × 15 days + tablet azithromycin 1 g single dose; Kit 5 (red) — genital ulcer disease, herpetic: tablet acyclovir 400 mg TDS × 7 days (HSV); Kit 6 (yellow) — lower abdominal pain (pelvic inflammatory disease): tablet cefixime 400 mg single dose + tablet metronidazole 400 mg BD × 14 days + capsule doxycycline 100 mg BD × 14 days; Kit 7 (black) — inguinal bubo (lymphogranuloma venereum, chancroid): capsule doxycycline 100 mg BD × 21 days + tablet azithromycin 1 g single dose (aspirate fluctuant buboes; do not incise); scrotal swelling — Kit 1 plus analgesia (epididymo-orchitis); genital warts — podophyllin, cryotherapy, imiquimod. Pregnant women: avoid doxycycline — use erythromycin/azithromycin; avoid metronidazole high dose in first trimester; penicillin is essential for syphilis in pregnancy (desensitise if allergic). Neonatal: ophthalmia neonatorum prophylaxis; congenital syphilis treatment with aqueous penicillin. Aetiological management where laboratory support is available (Gram stain, wet mount, RPR, NAAT) is preferred for precise therapy; ceftriaxone 500 mg–1 g IM is preferred for gonorrhoea in current international guidelines due to cefixime resistance.",
[["NACO STI kits",[["Kit (colour)","Syndrome","Drugs"],["1 (grey)","Urethral/cervical/anorectal discharge","Azithromycin 1 g + cefixime 400 mg"],["2 (green)","Vaginal discharge","Secnidazole 2 g + fluconazole 150 mg"],["3 (white)","Genital ulcer (non-herpetic)","Benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU + azithromycin 1 g"],["4 (blue)","Genital ulcer, penicillin allergy","Doxycycline 100 mg BD × 15 d + azithromycin 1 g"],["5 (red)","Herpetic ulcer","Acyclovir 400 mg TDS × 7 d"],["6 (yellow)","Lower abdominal pain (PID)","Cefixime 400 mg + metronidazole 400 mg BD × 14 d + doxycycline 100 mg BD × 14 d"],["7 (black)","Inguinal bubo","Doxycycline 100 mg BD × 21 d + azithromycin 1 g"]]],
["Syndromic management components",["Treat at first visit","Counselling and risk reduction","Condom promotion","Partner notification and treatment","HIV and syphilis testing","Follow-up"]]],
{c:"NACO's colour-coded STI kits enable rapid syndromic treatment at the first visit; combined with counselling, condoms, partner treatment and HIV/syphilis testing, they reduce transmission."});

Z("pharm11",8,"ph",["Scabicides (permethrin, ivermectin, benzyl benzoate) kill the scabies mite, and pediculicides (permethrin, malathion, ivermectin) kill lice; treatment must include all close contacts, and clothing and bedding should be washed.","The mite burrows under skin; we kill it with a nerve-poisoning cream applied to the whole body from neck down — and treat the whole family at once to stop ping-pong reinfection.",["Permethrin 5% cream: drug of choice for scabies (Na⁺ channel → mite paralysis); apply neck to toes, wash after 8–12 h, repeat after 7 days; safe in children > 2 months, pregnancy; 1% lotion for head lice.","Ivermectin oral 200 µg/kg, repeat after 7–14 days: crusted (Norwegian) scabies (with permethrin), institutional outbreaks, mass treatment; not < 15 kg/pregnancy.","Others: benzyl benzoate 25% (irritant), gamma benzene hexachloride/lindane 1% (neurotoxic — seizures; avoid children, pregnancy), crotamiton 10% (also antipruritic), sulfur ointment 5–10% (infants, pregnancy), malathion 0.5% (lice).","Measures: treat all household contacts simultaneously, wash clothes/bedding in hot water, antihistamines for itch (may persist 2–4 weeks); head lice — comb wet, repeat 7–10 days."],"Scabies: 'Permethrin 5%, neck to toes, repeat in a week, treat everyone'."],
"Scabies is an intensely pruritic infestation by the mite Sarcoptes scabiei var hominis, spread by close contact; crusted (Norwegian) scabies occurs in immunocompromised and elderly with huge mite burden. Pediculosis is infestation by lice — Pediculus humanus capitis (head louse), P. h. corporis (body louse — vector of typhus, trench fever, relapsing fever), Phthirus pubis (pubic/crab louse). Drugs: (1) permethrin — synthetic pyrethroid; acts on mite/louse nerve membrane Na⁺ channels, delaying repolarisation and causing paralysis and death; poorly absorbed through skin (< 2%), rapidly metabolised; 5% cream is the drug of choice for scabies — applied to the entire body from the neck down (including scalp, face in infants and elderly), under nails, genitals and between fingers/toes, left for 8–14 hours, then washed off; repeat after 7 days; safe in pregnancy, lactation and infants ≥ 2 months; 1% lotion/cream rinse for head lice (apply to damp hair for 10 min, repeat after 7–10 days); adverse effects — burning, stinging, itching, erythema; (2) ivermectin — oral 200 µg/kg single dose repeated after 7–14 days (no ovicidal action); indicated in crusted scabies (with topical permethrin and keratolytics, repeated doses 1, 2, 8, 9, 15 ± more), institutional outbreaks/mass drug administration, patients unable to apply topical therapy, treatment failure; topical 0.5% lotion for head lice; contraindicated in pregnancy, lactation, children < 15 kg; (3) benzyl benzoate 25% emulsion — applied neck down on 2–3 consecutive days (or twice 24 h apart); inexpensive; irritant (burning, dermatitis), dilute for children; (4) gamma benzene hexachloride (lindane) 1% lotion — organochlorine; CNS toxicity (seizures, aplastic anaemia) from percutaneous absorption, especially in infants, pregnancy, extensive skin damage — banned/restricted; (5) crotamiton 10% cream — scabicide with antipruritic action; applied daily for 2–5 days; less effective; (6) precipitated sulfur 5–10% in petrolatum — applied nightly for 3 nights; safe in infants and pregnancy; messy and malodorous; (7) malathion 0.5% lotion — organophosphate pediculicide (cholinesterase inhibitor in lice), ovicidal; flammable; head lice resistant to permethrin; (8) others for lice — spinosad 0.9%, benzyl alcohol 5%, dimeticone 4% (physical suffocation; no resistance), abametapir; oral cotrimoxazole (adjunct). General measures for scabies: treat all household members and close contacts simultaneously (even if asymptomatic); wash clothing, bedding and towels in hot water (> 60 °C) and dry in sun, or seal in plastic bags for 72 hours; trim nails; treat secondary bacterial infection (impetigo, pyoderma — risk of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis) with antibiotics; antihistamines and emollients for itching, which may persist 2–4 weeks after successful treatment (post-scabetic itch; mild topical steroid); nodular scabies — intralesional or potent topical steroid. Head lice: wet combing with fine-tooth nit comb, repeat treatment after 7–10 days to kill newly hatched lice, examine and treat family members with live lice, no need to shave head or exclude from school; eyelash infestation — petroleum jelly. Body lice: improve hygiene, laundering of clothes (lice live in seams) — treatment of body usually not needed; pubic lice — treat as STI (permethrin/ivermectin, screen partners and for other STIs).",
[["Scabicides",[["Drug","Application","Notes"],["Permethrin 5%","Neck down, 8–14 h, repeat day 7","Drug of choice; pregnancy, infants > 2 months"],["Ivermectin oral","200 µg/kg, repeat 7–14 d","Crusted scabies, outbreaks; not < 15 kg/pregnancy"],["Benzyl benzoate 25%","2–3 days","Irritant; dilute in children"],["Crotamiton 10%","Daily 2–5 days","Antipruritic; less effective"],["Sulfur 5–10%","3 nights","Infants, pregnancy"],["Lindane 1%","Single","Neurotoxic — avoid"]]],
["Pediculicides",[["Drug","Notes"],["Permethrin 1%","First-line for head lice; repeat 7–10 d"],["Malathion 0.5%","Ovicidal; flammable"],["Ivermectin (oral/topical 0.5%)","Resistant cases"],["Dimeticone 4%, benzyl alcohol, spinosad","Alternatives"]]],
["General measures",["Treat all contacts simultaneously","Hot-wash clothing and bedding","Antihistamines; itch may persist 2–4 weeks","Treat secondary infection","Wet combing for lice"]]],
{c:"Permethrin 5% is the scabicide of choice and ivermectin is used orally for crusted or mass treatment; success depends on correct application, repeat dosing and simultaneous treatment of contacts and fomites."});

Z("pharm11",9,"ph",["During the COVID-19 pandemic many drugs were tried; evidence now supports corticosteroids (dexamethasone) for patients needing oxygen, anticoagulation, IL-6 blockers (tocilizumab) or JAK inhibitors (baricitinib) in severe disease, and early antivirals (nirmatrelvir–ritonavir, remdesivir, molnupiravir) for high-risk patients — while hydroxychloroquine, ivermectin, azithromycin and convalescent plasma proved ineffective.","Early on the virus is the enemy (antivirals); later the body's own overreaction (inflammation, clotting) is the enemy (steroids, immunomodulators, anticoagulants).",["Antivirals: nirmatrelvir–ritonavir (3CL protease inhibitor; within 5 days, high-risk outpatients; CYP3A4 interactions), remdesivir (RdRp inhibitor; IV; hospitalised/early high-risk), molnupiravir (mutagenesis; limited).","Immunomodulators: dexamethasone 6 mg/day × 10 days (only if on oxygen — RECOVERY, ↓ mortality); tocilizumab (IL-6R) or baricitinib (JAK) in severe/progressing disease; avoid steroids in mild disease (mucormycosis risk with hyperglycaemia).","Anticoagulants: prophylactic LMWH/heparin in hospitalised patients; therapeutic in selected non-critical patients.","Not recommended: hydroxychloroquine, ivermectin, azithromycin, favipiravir, lopinavir/r, convalescent plasma, colchicine (routine); monoclonal antibodies lost activity against Omicron; vaccines — Covishield, Covaxin, mRNA."],"COVID severe: 'Dexamethasone + oxygen + anticoagulant ± tocilizumab/baricitinib'."],
"COVID-19, caused by SARS-CoV-2, ranges from asymptomatic infection to severe pneumonia, ARDS, thrombosis and multiorgan failure; disease has an early viral replication phase and a later hyperinflammatory phase, which guides therapy. Historical overview: (1) repurposed drugs that failed in randomised trials (RECOVERY, WHO SOLIDARITY) — hydroxychloroquine/chloroquine (no benefit; QT toxicity), lopinavir–ritonavir, interferon-β, azithromycin, ivermectin (no benefit in large trials), favipiravir (approved in India early; weak evidence), colchicine, convalescent plasma, itolizumab, nitazoxanide, vitamin D/zinc; widespread misuse contributed to adverse effects and COVID-associated mucormycosis (with steroids and hyperglycaemia) in India in 2021; (2) proven therapies — (a) antivirals: nirmatrelvir–ritonavir (Paxlovid; nirmatrelvir inhibits viral main protease Mpro/3CLpro, ritonavir boosts levels by CYP3A4 inhibition; 5-day oral course within 5 days of symptom onset in high-risk unvaccinated/elderly/immunocompromised outpatients; reduces hospitalisation ~89% in early trials; major drug interactions — statins, antiarrhythmics, anticoagulants, immunosuppressants; dysgeusia, rebound); remdesivir (adenosine nucleotide prodrug; inhibits RNA-dependent RNA polymerase by delayed chain termination; IV; 3-day course in high-risk outpatients reduces hospitalisation; in hospitalised patients on low-flow oxygen modest benefit; ADR — transaminase rise, bradycardia, infusion reactions; avoid in severe renal impairment historically); molnupiravir (oral; induces lethal mutagenesis via RdRp; modest efficacy; not for pregnancy; approved in India); (b) neutralising monoclonal antibodies (casirivimab–imdevimab, sotrovimab, bebtelovimab) — effective early against earlier variants but lost activity against Omicron subvariants; (c) corticosteroids — dexamethasone 6 mg/day oral or IV for up to 10 days in hospitalised patients requiring supplemental oxygen or ventilation reduced 28-day mortality (by one-third in ventilated patients — RECOVERY trial); harmful/no benefit in patients not requiring oxygen; (d) immunomodulators for patients with rapidly increasing oxygen needs and systemic inflammation — tocilizumab or sarilumab (IL-6 receptor antagonists) with steroids; baricitinib or tofacitinib (JAK inhibitors) — reduce mortality; risk of secondary infections; (e) anticoagulants — prophylactic-dose LMWH or UFH for all hospitalised patients (thromboprophylaxis; COVID is prothrombotic); therapeutic-dose heparin may benefit non-critically ill hospitalised patients with low bleeding risk; not routine after discharge; (f) supportive care — oxygen therapy, awake prone positioning, high-flow nasal oxygen, non-invasive and invasive ventilation, ECMO, fluid management, glycaemic control (avoid hyperglycaemia — mucormycosis), antipyretics (paracetamol), treatment of bacterial co-infection only when suspected; (g) mild disease — symptomatic treatment (paracetamol, hydration, rest), home isolation, monitoring of oxygen saturation, no antibiotics or steroids; (h) prevention — vaccines (Covishield — ChAdOx1 viral vector, Covaxin — inactivated whole virion, Corbevax — protein subunit, ZyCoV-D — DNA, mRNA vaccines globally) dramatically reduced severe disease; pre-exposure prophylaxis with long-acting monoclonal antibodies in immunocompromised (pemivibart). Lessons: importance of randomised controlled trials and evidence-based therapy over anecdotal repurposing; rational use of steroids and antibiotics.",
[["Evidence summary",[["Drug","Status","Indication"],["Nirmatrelvir–ritonavir","Effective","Early, high-risk outpatients (≤ 5 days)"],["Remdesivir","Effective (modest)","Early high-risk; hospitalised on low-flow O2"],["Molnupiravir","Modest","High-risk when others unavailable"],["Dexamethasone","Effective (↓ mortality)","Hospitalised on oxygen/ventilation"],["Tocilizumab / baricitinib","Effective","Severe, progressing with inflammation"],["Heparin/LMWH","Effective","Thromboprophylaxis (hospitalised)"],["Hydroxychloroquine, ivermectin, azithromycin, favipiravir, lopinavir/r, convalescent plasma","Ineffective","Not recommended"],["Monoclonal antibodies","Lost activity vs Omicron","—"]]],
["Phase-based approach",[["Phase","Approach"],["Early viral (mild, high-risk)","Antivirals"],["Moderate (hypoxia)","Dexamethasone + prophylactic anticoagulation ± remdesivir"],["Severe/critical","Dexamethasone + tocilizumab/baricitinib + ventilatory support"]]]],
{c:"COVID-19 pharmacotherapy evolved from failed repurposed drugs to an evidence-based, phase-specific approach: early antivirals for high-risk patients and corticosteroids, immunomodulators and anticoagulants for severe disease, with vaccines as the key preventive tool."});
